1
00:01:42,230 --> 00:01:45,480
-When'll you be back, Roy? -Oh, in a couple of weeks.

2
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:48,650
Don't do nothing you wouldn't want me to hear about.

3
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:51,030
-Well, so long. -So long.

4
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,570
How about a lift, mister?

5
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:04,250
-Can't you see that sticker? -Sure, l see it.

6
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:08,330
But a good guy don't pay no attention to

7
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:10,320
what some heel makes him stick on his truck.

8
00:02:10,390 --> 00:02:14,030
Well, scrunch down on the running board

9
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:16,220
till we get around the bend.

10
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:38,700
-Going far? -No, just a couple of miles.

11
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:43,420
l'd have walked her if my dogs wasn't pooped out.

12
00:02:43,510 --> 00:02:46,460
-Looking for ajob? -No. My old man's got a place.

13
00:02:47,470 --> 00:02:50,430
40 acres. He's a sharecropper, but we've been there a long while.

14
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:56,230
-Been doing ajob? -Yeah.

15
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,940
l seen your hands. You been swinging a pick or a sledge.

16
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:03,870
That's what makes them shiny.

17
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:05,720
l notice things like that all the time.

18
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:08,110
Got a trade?

19
00:03:08,790 --> 00:03:11,660
-Why don't you get at it, buddy? -Get at what?

20
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,690
You know. You been goin' over me since l got in.

21
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:16,050
Why don't you ask where l've been?

22
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:18,770
l don't stick my nose in nobody's business. l stay in my own yard.

23
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:22,050
That big nose of yours been goin' over me

24
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:23,970
like a sheep in a vegetable patch.

25
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:26,410
l ain't keepin' it secret.

26
00:03:26,410 --> 00:03:28,440
l've been in the penitentiary. Been there four years.

27
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:30,730
-Anything else? -You ain't gotta get sore.

28
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,810
-Ask me anything. -l didn't mean nothing.

29
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,010
Me neither.

30
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:38,410
l'm just trying to get along without shovin' anybody, that's all.

31
00:03:38,490 --> 00:03:42,240
See that road ahead? That's where l get out.

32
00:03:50,590 --> 00:03:51,290
You're about to bust a gut to know what l done, ain't you?

33
00:03:51,290 --> 00:03:56,540
l ain't a guy to let you down.

34
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:58,300
Homicide.

35
00:04:20,170 --> 00:04:23,870
Hmm, He's my saviour

36
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:27,330
Hmm, my saviour

37
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:31,960
Hmm, my saviour now

38
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:35,300
-Howdy, friend. -Howdy.

39
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:42,730
Ain't you young Tom Joad, old Tom's boy?

40
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:45,610
Yeah. l'm on my way home now.

41
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:48,020
Well, l do declare.

42
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:51,070
l baptised you, son.

43
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:53,650
Ain't you the preacher?

44
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:02,120
Used to be.Not no more.

45
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:04,290
l lost the call.

46
00:05:05,430 --> 00:05:08,600
But, boy, l sure used to have it.

47
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,180
l used to get an irrigation ditch so full of repented sinners

48
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:15,350
l'd pretty near drown half of 'em.

49
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:17,520
But not no more.

50
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:21,060
l lost the spirit.

51
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:27,820
l got nothing to preach about no more, that's all.

52
00:05:27,910 --> 00:05:30,150
l ain't so sure of things.

53
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:32,330
l remember you preaching a sermon

54
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:37,130
walking around on your hands, shoutin' your head off.

55
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:41,750
Yeah, l remember.Went pretty good that way.

56
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:43,920
But that was nothin'.

57
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:44,760
l preached a whole sermon once

58
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:47,760
straddling the ridge pole of a barn.

59
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:50,060
Like this.

60
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:55,950
-Did you see that one? -No.

61
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:59,400
You didn't?

62
00:06:01,950 --> 00:06:04,790
Well, it's all gone anyway.

63
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:08,040
You should have got yourself a wife.

64
00:06:08,130 --> 00:06:10,110
Why, at my meetings, l used to

65
00:06:10,110 --> 00:06:14,080
get the girls a-glory-shoutin' till they about passed out.

66
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:16,550
Then l'd go to comfort 'em.

67
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,640
l'd always end up by loving 'em.

68
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,390
l'd feel bad and pray and pray, but it didn't do no good.

69
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:26,810
Next time do it again.

70
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:29,900
l figured l just wasn't worth saving.

71
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:34,070
Pa always says you was never cut out for no preacher.

72
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:38,070
l never let one get by me if l could catch her. Have a snort?

73
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:43,580
But you wasn't a preacher. A girl was just a girl to you.

74
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:46,420
To me they's holy vessels.

75
00:06:47,470 --> 00:06:49,760
l was saving their souls.

76
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:57,220
l asked myself, what is this here called holy spirit?

77
00:06:57,310 --> 00:06:59,470
Maybe that's love.

78
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:04,850
Why, l love everybody so much l'm fit to burst sometimes.

79
00:07:06,610 --> 00:07:11,820
So... maybe there ain't no sin and there ain't no virtue.

80
00:07:11,910 --> 00:07:15,000
lt's just what people does.

81
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:22,760
Some things folks do is nice and some ain't so nice.

82
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:25,930
And that's all any man's got a right to say.

83
00:07:26,010 --> 00:07:31,050
Of course, l'll say a grace if somebody sets out the food.

84
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:33,260
But my heart ain't in it.

85
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:35,700
Nice drinkin' liQuor.

86
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:39,860
Ought to be. lt's factory liQuor. Cost a buck.

87
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:42,090
You been out travelling around?

88
00:07:42,090 --> 00:07:44,490
Ain't you heard? lt's been in the papers.

89
00:07:44,570 --> 00:07:46,770
No, l never. What?

90
00:07:46,770 --> 00:07:48,490
l've been in the penitentiary for four years.

91
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:50,660
Excuse me for asking.

92
00:07:53,750 --> 00:07:57,370
l don't mind no more. l'd do what l done again.

93
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:00,710
Killed a guy in a dance hall.

94
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:04,010
We was drunk.

95
00:08:04,010 --> 00:08:06,300
He got a knife in me and l laid him out with a shovel.

96
00:08:06,390 --> 00:08:10,060
-Knocked his head plumb to sQuash. -You ain't ashamed?

97
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,340
-No. He had a knife in me.

98
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:14,060
-That's why they only gave me seven years.

99
00:08:14,150 --> 00:08:16,770
l got out in four. Parole.

100
00:08:16,860 --> 00:08:20,810
-Ain't seen your folks since? -No, but l aim to before sundown.

101
00:08:20,910 --> 00:08:23,120
And l'm gettin' excited about it too.

102
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:26,070
-Which way are you goin'? -Oh, it don't matter.

103
00:08:26,750 --> 00:08:27,530
Ever since l lost the spirit,

104
00:08:27,530 --> 00:08:28,710
looks like l'd just as soon go one way as the other.

105
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:32,750
l'll go your way.

106
00:08:34,930 --> 00:08:37,710
Maybe Ma will have pork for supper.

107
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:42,800
l ain't had pork but four times in four years. Every Christmas.

108
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:45,270
l'll be glad to see your pa.

109
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:49,100
Last time l seen him was at a baptising.

110
00:08:49,190 --> 00:08:53,060
He had one of the biggest doses of the holy spirit l ever seen.

111
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:57,950
Got to jumping over bushes. Howlin' like a dog-wolf at moon-time.

112
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:02,500
Finally, he picks himself out a bush big as a piano

113
00:09:02,580 --> 00:09:06,080
and he lets out a sQuawk and takes a run at that bush.

114
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:21,470
Well, he cleared her.

115
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:24,770
But he bust his leg snap in two doing it.

116
00:09:25,610 --> 00:09:28,620
There was a travelling dentist and he set her

117
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:30,020
and l gave her a praying over, but...

118
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:34,990
there wasn't no more holy spirit left in your pa after that.

119
00:09:37,710 --> 00:09:42,750
-Listen. That wind's fixing to do something. -Sure it is.

120
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:45,210
Always is this time of year.

121
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:18,800
Ma?

122
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:21,970
Pa?

123
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,680
Ma?

124
00:10:27,730 --> 00:10:29,800
Ain't nobody here.

125
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:35,270
-Something's happened. -You got a match?

126
00:10:56,050 --> 00:10:58,420
They're all gone or dead.

127
00:10:58,510 --> 00:11:04,140
-They never wrote you nothing? -No. They wasn't people to write.

128
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:13,690
lt's Ma's. She had 'em for years.

129
00:11:34,890 --> 00:11:39,350
Used to be mine. l gave it to Grandpa when l went away.

130
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:48,610
You reckon they're dead?

131
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:52,950
l never heard nothing about it.

132
00:11:57,750 --> 00:11:59,830
(man) Tommy?

133
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,430
Muley.

134
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:10,300
-Where's my folks, Muley? -Why, they gone.

135
00:12:10,390 --> 00:12:13,680
l know they're gone, but where are they gone?

136
00:12:13,770 --> 00:12:17,100
lt's Muley Graves. You remember the preacher, don't you?

137
00:12:17,190 --> 00:12:20,940
-l ain't no preacher any more. -All right. You remember the man?

138
00:12:21,030 --> 00:12:23,950
-Glad to see you again. -Now, where are my folks?

139
00:12:24,030 --> 00:12:24,780
They gone. They gone to your Uncle John's.

140
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:29,540
The whole crowd of 'em. Two weeks ago.

141
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:33,040
But they can't stay there, cos John's got his notice to get off.

142
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:36,160
What happened? How come they gotta get off?

143
00:12:36,250 --> 00:12:39,120
We lived here 50 years, same place.

144
00:12:39,630 --> 00:12:42,680
Everybody's gotta get off. Everybody's leaving.

145
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:44,210
Going out to California.

146
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,840
Your folks, my folks, everybody's folks.

147
00:12:47,930 --> 00:12:51,390
Everybody except me. l ain't getting off!

148
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:54,350
-Who done it? -Listen.

149
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:56,890
That's some of what done it.

150
00:12:56,890 --> 00:12:59,350
The dusters. They started it, anyways.

151
00:12:59,450 --> 00:13:02,150
Blowin' like this year after year.

152
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:05,450
Blowin' the land away. Blowin' the crops away!

153
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:08,110
Blowin' us away now.

154
00:13:09,170 --> 00:13:11,240
Are you crazy?

155
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:14,290
Some say l am. You wanna hear how it happened?

156
00:13:14,380 --> 00:13:16,870
That's what l'm asking you, ain't it?

157
00:13:16,970 --> 00:13:20,050
Well... the way it happens,

158
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:22,930
the way it happened to me...

159
00:13:23,020 --> 00:13:24,760
A man come one day...

160
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:27,810
After what them dusters done to the land,

161
00:13:27,810 --> 00:13:30,270
the tenant system don't work no more.

162
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,930
They don't break even, much less show profit.

163
00:13:33,030 --> 00:13:36,480
One man and a tractor can handle 12 or 14 of these places.

164
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:38,950
You just pay him a wage and take all the crop.

165
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:44,280
Yeah, but we couldn't do on any less than what our share is now.

166
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:46,990
The children ain't getting enough to eat as it is.

167
00:13:46,990 --> 00:13:48,950
And they're so ragged,

168
00:13:49,050 --> 00:13:52,580
we'd be ashamed if everybody else's children wasn't the same.

169
00:13:52,680 --> 00:13:55,180
l can't help that. l got my orders.

170
00:13:55,180 --> 00:13:56,430
They told me to tell you to get off.

171
00:13:56,510 --> 00:13:59,680
-That's what l'm tellin' you. -You mean get off my own land?

172
00:13:59,770 --> 00:14:03,190
-Don't go to blaming me. lt ain't my fault. -Whose fault is it?

173
00:14:03,270 --> 00:14:06,610
You know who owns the land. Shawnee Land and Cattle Company.

174
00:14:06,690 --> 00:14:10,360
-Who's Shawnee Land and Cattle Company? -Nobody. lt's a company.

175
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:12,210
They got a president.

176
00:14:12,210 --> 00:14:14,860
They got somebody who knows what a shotgun's for!

177
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:16,830
Oh, son, it ain't his fault

178
00:14:16,830 --> 00:14:18,490
because the bank tells him what to do.

179
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:20,740
All right. Where's the bank?

180
00:14:20,740 --> 00:14:22,910
Tulsa. What's the use of picking on him?

181
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:24,510
He ain't nothing but the manager.

182
00:14:24,510 --> 00:14:27,550
He's half crazy trying to keep up with his orders.

183
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:31,050
-Then who do we shoot? -Brother, l don't know.

184
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:34,840
lf l did, l'd tell you. l just don't know who's to blame.

185
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:37,410
l'm right here to tell you,

186
00:14:37,410 --> 00:14:39,270
mister: there ain't nobody gonna push me off my land!

187
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:42,690
My grandpa took up this land 70 years ago!

188
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:46,330
My pa was born here.

189
00:14:46,330 --> 00:14:48,820
We was all born on it! And some of us was killed on it!

190
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:01,710
And some of us... died on it.

191
00:15:02,890 --> 00:15:05,090
That's what makes it our'n.

192
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:07,510
Being born on it,

193
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:10,550
and workin' on it...

194
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:14,600
and dying... dying on it!

195
00:15:14,690 --> 00:15:18,610
And not no piece of paper with writing on it...

196
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:33,580
Well, what happened?

197
00:15:36,340 --> 00:15:40,590
They come. They come and pushed me off.

198
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:42,840
-They come with the cats. -The what?

199
00:15:42,930 --> 00:15:46,140
The cats, the caterpillar tractors.

200
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,140
And for every one of 'em,

201
00:16:10,140 --> 00:16:14,800
there was ten, 15 families thrown right out of their homes.

202
00:16:14,890 --> 00:16:19,800
100 folks. And no place to live but on the road.

203
00:16:19,890 --> 00:16:24,350
The Rances, the Peterses, the Perrys, the Joadses...

204
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:27,730
One right after the other, they got thrown out.

205
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:32,530
Half the folks you and me know thrown right out into the road.

206
00:16:34,330 --> 00:16:37,950
The one that got me come about a month ago.

207
00:16:38,870 --> 00:16:40,830
Go on back!

208
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:45,420
Go on back! l'm warning you! Go on back!

209
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:58,690
You come any closer and l'm gonna blow you right out of that cat!

210
00:16:58,770 --> 00:17:01,350
l told you!

211
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:05,010
You're Joe Davis' boy.

212
00:17:05,010 --> 00:17:07,110
l don't like for nobody to draw a bead on me.

213
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:10,370
Then what are you doing this for? Against your own people.

214
00:17:10,450 --> 00:17:13,240
Three dollars a day, that's what l'm doing it for.

215
00:17:13,330 --> 00:17:15,810
l got two little kids at home.

216
00:17:15,820 --> 00:17:18,620
My wife. My wife's mother. Them folks gotta eat.

217
00:17:18,710 --> 00:17:21,030
First and only, l think about my folks

218
00:17:21,030 --> 00:17:23,210
What happens to others is their own lookout.

219
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:26,750
Yeah, but you don't understand, son. This is my land!

220
00:17:26,850 --> 00:17:29,140
Used to be. lt's the company's now.

221
00:17:29,230 --> 00:17:31,230
Have it your own way, son.

222
00:17:31,230 --> 00:17:34,390
Butjust as sure as you touch my house with that cat,

223
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:37,480
l'm gonna blow you plumb to kingdom come!

224
00:17:37,570 --> 00:17:39,700
You ain't gonna blow nobody nowhere!

225
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:42,070
First place, they'd hang you and you know it.

226
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:43,310
lt wouldn't be two days

227
00:17:43,310 --> 00:17:45,610
before they'd send a guy to take my place.

228
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:48,490
Now go on! Get outta the way!

229
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:31,090
What was the use? He was right.

230
00:18:31,170 --> 00:18:35,170
There wasn't a thing in the world l could do about it.

231
00:18:35,260 --> 00:18:38,260
lt's just... it don't seem possible

232
00:18:38,260 --> 00:18:41,270
just getting throwed off like that.

233
00:18:41,350 --> 00:18:44,140
The rest of my family set out for the West.

234
00:18:44,230 --> 00:18:46,310
There wasn't nothing to eat,

235
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:50,730
but... l couldn't leave. Something just wouldn't let me.

236
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:56,200
So now l just wander around and sleep wherever l am.

237
00:18:59,170 --> 00:19:02,330
l used to tell myself that l was looking out for things

238
00:19:02,420 --> 00:19:07,330
so that when the folks come back, everything would be all right.

239
00:19:07,430 --> 00:19:09,960
But l knowed it wasn't true.

240
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:13,560
There ain't nothin' to look out for.

241
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:17,060
And there ain't nobody ever coming back.

242
00:19:17,140 --> 00:19:19,220
They're gone!

243
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:22,430
And me...

244
00:19:22,530 --> 00:19:25,900
l'm just an old graveyard ghost.

245
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:30,820
That's all in the world l am.

246
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:35,740
Do you think l'm touched?

247
00:19:40,670 --> 00:19:42,750
No.

248
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:45,550
You're lonely, but...

249
00:19:45,640 --> 00:19:48,640
you ain't touched.

250
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:50,300
Well, it don't matter.

251
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:53,470
lf l'm touched, l'm touched and that's all there is to it.

252
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:58,190
The thing l don't understand is my folks taking it. Like Ma.

253
00:19:58,280 --> 00:20:01,900
l seen her nearly beat a pedlar to death with a live chicken.

254
00:20:01,990 --> 00:20:05,450
She aimed to go with an axe in the other hand, got mixed up

255
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:06,620
forgot which was which.

256
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:08,360
When she got through with that pedlar,

257
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:11,700
all she had left was two chicken legs.

258
00:20:11,790 --> 00:20:13,620
Just a...

259
00:20:13,710 --> 00:20:17,210
Just a plain old graveyard ghost.

260
00:20:18,510 --> 00:20:20,590
That's all.

261
00:20:34,860 --> 00:20:36,940
She's settling.

262
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:43,830
What do you figure on doing?

263
00:20:43,910 --> 00:20:45,990
lt's hard to say.

264
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:50,210
Stay here till morning, go to Uncle John's, l reckon.

265
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:52,870
After that, l don't know.

266
00:20:54,390 --> 00:20:56,040
Listen.

267
00:21:03,650 --> 00:21:06,930
That's them. Them lights.

268
00:21:07,030 --> 00:21:09,560
Come on. Come on. We gotta hide out.

269
00:21:09,650 --> 00:21:13,020
Hide out for what? We ain't doing nothing.

270
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:14,950
You're trespassing.

271
00:21:14,950 --> 00:21:17,060
This ain't your land no more!

272
00:21:17,060 --> 00:21:18,410
That's the superintendent with a gun.

273
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:22,120
-Come on! -Come on, Tom. You're on parole.

274
00:21:45,030 --> 00:21:46,900
Muley!

275
00:22:01,130 --> 00:22:04,340
All you gotta do is hide and watch.

276
00:22:04,430 --> 00:22:08,180
-Won't they come out here? -(chuckles) l don't think so.

277
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:11,050
One came out once and l clipped him...

278
00:22:11,140 --> 00:22:13,350
..from behind with a fence stake.

279
00:22:13,440 --> 00:22:15,760
They ain't bothered since.

280
00:22:20,450 --> 00:22:22,520
He ain't here.

281
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:36,790
Anybody ever told me l'd be hiding out in my own place...

282
00:22:52,230 --> 00:22:54,850
Lord, make us grateful for

283
00:22:54,850 --> 00:22:58,600
what we are about to receive, for His sake. Amen.

284
00:22:58,700 --> 00:23:01,150
l seen you. You ate during grace.

285
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:05,290
Just one little dab. Just one teeny little old dab. That's all.

286
00:23:05,370 --> 00:23:07,600
Ain't he messy though.

287
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:09,840
l seen him. Gobbling away like an old pig.

288
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:12,710
Why don't you keep your eyes shut during grace?

289
00:23:12,800 --> 00:23:15,120
What's it say again, Uncle John?

290
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:22,020
lt says, ''Plenty of work in California. 800 pickers wanted.''

291
00:23:27,320 --> 00:23:29,080
Wait till l get to California.

292
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:32,900
l'm gonna reach up and pick me an orange whenever l want it.

293
00:23:32,990 --> 00:23:35,110
Or some grapes. Now-now-now,

294
00:23:35,110 --> 00:23:37,780
there's something l ain't never had enough of.

295
00:23:37,870 --> 00:23:41,490
l'm gonna get me a whole big bunch of grapes off a bush

296
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:43,840
and l'm gonna sQuash 'em all over my face

297
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:46,870
and let the juice drain down off of my chin.

298
00:23:46,970 --> 00:23:49,420
Praise the Lord for victory!

299
00:23:49,510 --> 00:23:52,800
Maybe l'll get me a whole washtub full of grapes

300
00:23:52,890 --> 00:23:56,090
and just sit in 'em and scrounge around in 'em

301
00:23:56,090 --> 00:23:58,760
until they're all gone.

302
00:24:00,190 --> 00:24:07,520
l sure would like that. Yes, sir, l sure would like that.

303
00:24:27,220 --> 00:24:29,760
Oh, thank God. Thank God.

304
00:24:30,890 --> 00:24:32,970
Tommy.

305
00:24:37,770 --> 00:24:39,850
Ma.

306
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:48,960
You didn't bust out? You ain't gotta hide

307
00:24:48,960 --> 00:24:51,370
No, Ma. l'm paroled. l got my papers.

308
00:24:54,090 --> 00:24:57,670
l was just scared we was going away without you

309
00:24:57,670 --> 00:25:00,540
and we'd never see each other again.

310
00:25:00,630 --> 00:25:03,130
l'd have found you, Ma.

311
00:25:07,180 --> 00:25:09,590
Muley told me what happened.

312
00:25:09,690 --> 00:25:11,760
We going to California true?

313
00:25:11,860 --> 00:25:14,690
We've gotta go, Tommy, but it's gonna be all right.

314
00:25:14,780 --> 00:25:18,360
l seen the handbills about how much work there is,

315
00:25:18,360 --> 00:25:19,740
and high wages too.

316
00:25:20,910 --> 00:25:26,120
There's something l gotta find out first, Tommy. Did they hurt you, son?

317
00:25:26,210 --> 00:25:29,740
-Did they hurt you and make you mean mad? -Mad, Ma?

318
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:31,690
Sometimes they do.

319
00:25:31,690 --> 00:25:33,700
No, Ma. l was at first, but not no more.

320
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:36,340
Sometimes they do something to you.

321
00:25:36,430 --> 00:25:39,630
They hurt you and you get mad and then you get mean.

322
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:42,840
And they hurt you again and you get meaner and meaner,

323
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:51,120
Did they hurt you that way, son?

324
00:25:51,120 --> 00:25:52,530
No, Ma. Don't worry about that.

325
00:25:52,610 --> 00:25:56,610
Well, l...l don't want no mean son.

326
00:25:56,950 --> 00:25:59,740
lt's Tommy. lt's Tommy back.

327
00:25:59,830 --> 00:26:02,120
What did you do, son? Bust out?

328
00:26:02,210 --> 00:26:04,290
Tommy's out ofjail!

329
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:05,830
l... l knowed it! You couldn't keep him in.

330
00:26:05,830 --> 00:26:09,720
You can't keep a Joad in jail.

331
00:26:09,800 --> 00:26:12,210
l knowed it from the first.

332
00:26:12,300 --> 00:26:13,660
Get out of my way!

333
00:26:13,660 --> 00:26:18,090
l told you so. l told you Tom would come bustin' out of thatjail

334
00:26:18,190 --> 00:26:20,640
just like a bull through corral fence.

335
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:22,850
You can't keep a Joad in jail.

336
00:26:22,940 --> 00:26:26,610
-l didn't bust out. They paroled me. -l was that way myself.

337
00:26:26,700 --> 00:26:31,690
-How are you, Uncle John? -Hello, Tommy. l'm feeling fine.

338
00:26:31,780 --> 00:26:36,030
-How are you, Noah? -Fine, Tommy. Bust out?

339
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:38,410
No, parole.

340
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:40,580
-Hello. -Tommy.

341
00:26:43,550 --> 00:26:47,330
The jailbird's back! The jailbird's back!

342
00:26:52,270 --> 00:26:55,270
-Hiya, Al. -Hello, Tom.

343
00:26:55,850 --> 00:26:59,560
-Did you bust out ofjail? -No, they paroled me.

344
00:26:59,650 --> 00:27:02,140
Rosasharn.

345
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:04,150
Busted out.

346
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:07,410
That's Connie Rivers with her. They're married now.

347
00:27:07,490 --> 00:27:10,910
She's due now about three, four months.

348
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,660
She wasn't any more than a kid when l went up.

349
00:27:15,500 --> 00:27:18,840
-Hiya, Rosasharn. -How are you, Tom?

350
00:27:19,630 --> 00:27:22,750
This is Connie, my husband.

351
00:27:24,140 --> 00:27:26,300
-Did you... -No, parole.

352
00:27:27,350 --> 00:27:29,680
Well, if this don't beat all.

353
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:32,010
l see l'm going to be an uncle soon.

354
00:27:32,110 --> 00:27:34,180
Oh, you do not see.

355
00:27:34,280 --> 00:27:39,270
-Look at her blush. Look at her blush. -Look at her blushing.

356
00:27:47,790 --> 00:27:50,710
Hey, Joad! John Joad.

357
00:27:58,390 --> 00:28:01,170
-You ain't forgot, have you? -We ain't forgot.

358
00:28:01,270 --> 00:28:05,020
-We'll be coming through here tomorrow. -l know.

359
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:07,970
We be out. We be out by sun-up.

360
00:28:25,210 --> 00:28:27,630
How did you get all this money?

361
00:28:27,630 --> 00:28:31,170
Sold things. Chopped cotton. Even Grandpa.

362
00:28:31,260 --> 00:28:36,680
Got us about $200 all told. Shucked out 75 for this here truck.

363
00:28:36,770 --> 00:28:40,480
Still got nearly 150 to set out on.

364
00:28:40,480 --> 00:28:41,840
l figure we ought to be able to make her on that.

365
00:28:41,940 --> 00:28:45,890
Easy. After all, they ain't but about 12 of us, is they?

366
00:28:49,990 --> 00:28:52,290
l reckon we better begin rousting them out

367
00:28:52,290 --> 00:28:54,240
if we aim to get out by daylight.

368
00:28:54,330 --> 00:28:56,570
How about it, John? How you boys coming?

369
00:30:28,650 --> 00:30:30,110
Ma.

370
00:30:30,190 --> 00:30:32,270
l'm ready.

371
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:45,670
Rosasharn, honey. Wake up the children. We're fixing to leave.

372
00:30:46,960 --> 00:30:49,420
Ruthie. Winfield. Jump up now.

373
00:30:50,590 --> 00:30:52,990
Where's Grandpa? Al, go get him.

374
00:30:52,990 --> 00:30:55,670
l'm gonna get up front. Somebody help me.

375
00:30:55,770 --> 00:30:57,260
Wait.

376
00:30:57,350 --> 00:31:00,800
-Kids, you climb up on top first. -We're going to California!

377
00:31:00,890 --> 00:31:01,990
Al's gonna drive,

378
00:31:01,990 --> 00:31:04,760
Ma. Sit with him and Grandma and we'll swap later.

379
00:31:04,860 --> 00:31:09,650
Connie. Help Rosasharn up there alongside the kids.

380
00:31:10,620 --> 00:31:13,530
-Where's Grandpa? -Grandpa!

381
00:31:13,620 --> 00:31:16,540
Where he always is, probably.

382
00:31:18,920 --> 00:31:21,670
Grandpa! Grandpa!

383
00:31:21,750 --> 00:31:25,340
Save him a place. John, you and Noah climb up and find a place.

384
00:31:25,420 --> 00:31:28,510
Got to kind of keep her even all around.

385
00:31:37,940 --> 00:31:40,110
Think it'll hold?

386
00:31:40,110 --> 00:31:43,100
lf it does, it'll be a miracle out of Scripture.

387
00:31:43,200 --> 00:31:45,240
Ma. Pa.

388
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:49,240
Let go of me, goldurn you! Dang you!

389
00:31:49,330 --> 00:31:52,530
Please. There's something the matter with him.

390
00:31:52,620 --> 00:31:57,540
-Why don't you stand still! -There's something wrong with him.

391
00:32:00,170 --> 00:32:04,120
-You let me alone, that's all. -That's all what?

392
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,310
What's the matter, Grandpa?

393
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:11,470
What's the matter? There's nothing the matter. l just...

394
00:32:11,560 --> 00:32:15,150
-l just ain't going, that's all. -What do you mean? We gotta go.

395
00:32:15,230 --> 00:32:17,310
We got no place to stay.

396
00:32:17,400 --> 00:32:21,860
l ain't talking about you. l'm talking about me.

397
00:32:21,950 --> 00:32:26,950
l give her a good going over all last night and l'm staying.

398
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:29,240
But you can't do that, Grandpa.

399
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:31,250
This here land's going under the tractor.

400
00:32:31,340 --> 00:32:35,670
-We all got to get out. -All except me and l'm staying.

401
00:32:35,760 --> 00:32:39,340
-What about Grandma? -Take her with you!

402
00:32:39,430 --> 00:32:43,010
Who'd cook for you, Grandpa? How are you gonna live?

403
00:32:43,100 --> 00:32:47,730
Muley's living, ain't he? And l'm twice the man that Muley is.

404
00:32:47,810 --> 00:32:51,150
Now, listen to me, Grandpa. Listen to me just a minute.

405
00:32:51,230 --> 00:32:53,440
And l ain't listening either.

406
00:32:53,530 --> 00:32:56,970
l told you what l was gonna do

407
00:32:56,980 --> 00:32:58,950
and l don't give a hoot and a holler

408
00:32:59,040 --> 00:33:02,900
if there's oranges and grapes crowding a fella out of bed.

409
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:06,370
l ain't going to California.

410
00:33:06,460 --> 00:33:11,540
Goldurn! This is my country and l belong here.

411
00:33:11,630 --> 00:33:13,920
Yes, sir.

412
00:33:14,010 --> 00:33:16,930
lt's my dirt.

413
00:33:17,020 --> 00:33:20,050
lt's no good, but it's...

414
00:33:20,140 --> 00:33:23,260
it's mine, all mine.

415
00:33:23,360 --> 00:33:26,720
Either we got to tie him up and throw him in the truck,

416
00:33:26,720 --> 00:33:28,400
or something. He can't stay here.

417
00:33:28,490 --> 00:33:29,730
No, can't tie him.

418
00:33:29,730 --> 00:33:33,730
Either we'll hurt him, or he'll get so mad, he'll hurt himself.

419
00:33:33,830 --> 00:33:37,110
-Reckon we could get him drunk? -Ain't no whiskey, is there?

420
00:33:37,210 --> 00:33:42,250
Now, wait. There's a half a bottle of soothing syrup here.

421
00:33:44,460 --> 00:33:47,880
Here. Used to put the children to sleep.

422
00:33:49,970 --> 00:33:53,420
-Don't taste bad. -There's coffee left. We can fix him a cup.

423
00:33:53,520 --> 00:33:57,050
-That's right, douse some in it. -Better give him a good dose.

424
00:33:57,150 --> 00:33:59,850
He's mighty muleheaded.

425
00:33:59,940 --> 00:34:01,600
lf Muley...

426
00:34:01,690 --> 00:34:05,060
lf Muley can scramble along, l...

427
00:34:05,160 --> 00:34:07,230
l guess l can.

428
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:11,740
l smell spareribs.

429
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:18,080
Somebody's been eating spareribs. How come l ain't got none?

430
00:34:18,170 --> 00:34:21,960
Well, l got some saved for you, Grandpa. Got some warming now.

431
00:34:22,050 --> 00:34:25,830
But here's a cup of coffee for you first.

432
00:34:25,930 --> 00:34:28,880
Get me a mess of spareribs.

433
00:34:28,980 --> 00:34:32,930
l want a great big mess of spareribs.

434
00:34:33,020 --> 00:34:37,570
-l'm... l'm hungry. -Why, sure you're hungry.

435
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:51,160
l... sure do like spareribs.

436
00:34:58,680 --> 00:35:02,920
-Get up there, Noah. -Put his feet in there first, Tom.

437
00:35:03,770 --> 00:35:05,840
Easy, now. Easy!

438
00:35:07,770 --> 00:35:11,470
Better throw something over him so he won't get sunstruck.

439
00:35:11,570 --> 00:35:15,810
Everything all set now? All right, let her go, Al.

440
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:20,190
Get aboard, Ma.

441
00:35:20,290 --> 00:35:24,330
-Well, goodbye and good luck. -Hold her, Al.

442
00:35:24,420 --> 00:35:26,790
Ain't you goin' with us?

443
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:28,620
l'd like to.

444
00:35:28,710 --> 00:35:31,690
There's something going on out there in the West

445
00:35:31,690 --> 00:35:34,090
and l'd like to try and learn what it is.

446
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:36,880
lf you feel you've got the room.

447
00:35:38,970 --> 00:35:41,100
Plenty of room. Get on.

448
00:35:41,190 --> 00:35:45,140
-Let her go, Al! -California, here we come!

449
00:36:04,760 --> 00:36:08,040
Ain't you gonna look back, Ma? Give the old place a last look?

450
00:36:08,130 --> 00:36:10,090
We're going to California, ain't we?

451
00:36:10,090 --> 00:36:12,300
All right then, let's go to California.

452
00:36:12,390 --> 00:36:15,510
That don't sound like you. You never was like that before.

453
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:19,050
l never had my house pushed over before.

454
00:36:19,150 --> 00:36:22,070
Never had my family stuck out on the road.

455
00:36:22,150 --> 00:36:25,770
Never had to lose everything l had in life.

456
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:40,780
-lt's gonna be all right, Grandpa. -l ain't going.

457
00:37:40,870 --> 00:37:44,240
l ain't going. l ain't going.

458
00:37:44,330 --> 00:37:47,040
l ain't going.

459
00:37:49,420 --> 00:37:51,630
lt's all right, Grandpa.

460
00:37:51,720 --> 00:37:54,420
You're just tired, that's all.

461
00:37:55,260 --> 00:37:57,590
That's it.

462
00:37:57,680 --> 00:37:59,970
Just tired.

463
00:38:01,810 --> 00:38:03,690
Just...

464
00:38:04,520 --> 00:38:06,400
tired.

465
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:42,290
"This here is William James Joad.

466
00:38:42,290 --> 00:38:44,680
Died of a stroke, old, old man."

467
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:50,030
''His folks buried him

468
00:38:50,030 --> 00:38:52,490
because they got no money to pay for funeral...s.''

469
00:38:59,460 --> 00:39:03,540
''Nobody killed him. Just a stroke and he died.''

470
00:39:07,470 --> 00:39:11,260
l figure best we leave something like this on him,

471
00:39:11,350 --> 00:39:16,430
'lest somebody digs him up and makes out he was killed.

472
00:39:16,520 --> 00:39:19,590
Looks like a lot of times the government's got more interest in

473
00:39:19,590 --> 00:39:21,650
a dead man than a live one.

474
00:39:21,740 --> 00:39:25,320
Not be so lonesome, knowing his name's there with him.

475
00:39:25,410 --> 00:39:29,320
Notjust an old fella lonesome underground.

476
00:39:31,210 --> 00:39:34,580
Would you say a few words, Casy?

477
00:39:34,670 --> 00:39:37,790
l ain't a preacher no more, you know.

478
00:39:37,880 --> 00:39:40,770
We know, but ain't none of our folks

479
00:39:40,770 --> 00:39:43,670
ever been buried without a few words.

480
00:39:46,140 --> 00:39:48,630
l'll say 'em, make it short.

481
00:39:55,110 --> 00:39:57,690
This here old man just...

482
00:39:57,780 --> 00:40:00,900
lived a life and just died out of it.

483
00:40:00,990 --> 00:40:04,490
l don't know whether he was good or bad.

484
00:40:04,580 --> 00:40:06,870
lt don't matter much.

485
00:40:07,880 --> 00:40:10,750
Heard a fella say a poem once.

486
00:40:10,840 --> 00:40:14,040
And he says, ''All that lives is holy.''

487
00:40:15,380 --> 00:40:18,640
Well, l wouldn't pray just for an old man that's dead,

488
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:21,390
cos he's all right.

489
00:40:21,480 --> 00:40:24,370
lf l was to pray, l'd pray for folks

490
00:40:24,370 --> 00:40:27,600
that's alive and don't know which way to turn.

491
00:40:27,690 --> 00:40:30,480
Grandpa here...

492
00:40:30,570 --> 00:40:33,940
he ain't got no more trouble like that.

493
00:40:34,030 --> 00:40:37,090
He's got his job all cut out for him,

494
00:40:37,090 --> 00:40:40,150
so cover him up and let him get to it.

495
00:41:32,060 --> 00:41:43,900
l'm going down the road feeling bad

496
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:51,150
l'm going down the road feeling bad, oh lordy

497
00:41:51,250 --> 00:41:57,040
l ain't going to be treated this way

498
00:41:58,220 --> 00:42:10,930
They fed me on corn bread and beans

499
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:15,570
Gosh, Connie sure sings pretty, don't he?

500
00:42:15,650 --> 00:42:17,980
Oh lordy

501
00:42:18,070 --> 00:42:24,110
l ain't going to be treated this way

502
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:32,460
-That's my son-in-law. -Sings real nice. What state you all from?

503
00:42:32,550 --> 00:42:36,550
Oklahoma. Had us a farm there, sharecropping.

504
00:42:36,640 --> 00:42:39,390
We're from Arkansas.

505
00:42:39,470 --> 00:42:44,100
Had me a store there. Kind of a general notions store.

506
00:42:44,190 --> 00:42:47,720
When the farms went, the stores went too.

507
00:42:48,570 --> 00:42:52,480
l had as nice a little store as you ever saw.

508
00:42:52,570 --> 00:42:55,740
l sure did hate to give it up.

509
00:42:56,200 --> 00:42:58,530
Well, you can't tell.

510
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:01,540
l figure when we get out there and get work,

511
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:04,350
maybe get us a piece of growing land near water,

512
00:43:04,350 --> 00:43:06,000
it might not be so bad at that.

513
00:43:06,090 --> 00:43:08,880
That's right. Paying good wages, l hear.

514
00:43:08,970 --> 00:43:12,670
-We can all get work. -Can't be no worse than home.

515
00:43:12,760 --> 00:43:15,050
You all must have a pot of money.

516
00:43:15,140 --> 00:43:16,240
No, we ain't got no money,

517
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:20,100
but there's plenty of us to work and we're all good men.

518
00:43:20,190 --> 00:43:22,990
Get good wages out there and put it all together

519
00:43:22,990 --> 00:43:25,400
and we'll be all right.

520
00:43:25,490 --> 00:43:28,610
Good wages, eh? Picking oranges and peaches?

521
00:43:28,700 --> 00:43:31,350
Well, we aim to take whatever they got.

522
00:43:31,350 --> 00:43:32,910
What's so funny about that?

523
00:43:32,990 --> 00:43:36,000
What's so funny about it? l've just been out there.

524
00:43:36,080 --> 00:43:39,910
l've been and seen it. l'm going back and starve

525
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:42,670
because l'd rather starve all over at once.

526
00:43:42,760 --> 00:43:44,440
What do you think you're talking about?

527
00:43:44,440 --> 00:43:48,380
l got a handbill says they're paying good wages.

528
00:43:48,470 --> 00:43:50,760
l seen in the papers they need pickers.

529
00:43:50,850 --> 00:43:53,600
All right, go on. Nobody's stopping you.

530
00:43:53,690 --> 00:43:57,100
-Yeah, but what about this? -l ain't gonna rile you. Go on.

531
00:43:57,190 --> 00:44:00,010
Wait a minute, buddy. You just done some jackassin'.

532
00:44:00,010 --> 00:44:02,320
You can't shut up now.

533
00:44:02,400 --> 00:44:02,950
lt says they need 800 pickers.

534
00:44:02,950 --> 00:44:06,730
You laugh and say they don't. Which one's the liar?

535
00:44:06,830 --> 00:44:10,410
-How many of you all got them handbills? -l got one.

536
00:44:10,500 --> 00:44:13,330
-l got one. -We all got one.

537
00:44:13,420 --> 00:44:19,010
-What does that prove? -There you are. Same yellow handbill.

538
00:44:19,090 --> 00:44:21,130
800 pickers wanted.

539
00:44:21,220 --> 00:44:26,010
All right, the man wants 800 men. So he prints 5,000 handbills

540
00:44:26,100 --> 00:44:28,420
and maybe 20,000 people see 'em.

541
00:44:28,520 --> 00:44:29,740
And maybe two or 3,000 people

542
00:44:29,740 --> 00:44:32,810
start west on account of that handbill.

543
00:44:32,900 --> 00:44:36,270
Two or 3,000 people that are crazy with worry

544
00:44:36,360 --> 00:44:40,060
heading out for 800 jobs. Now, does that make sense?

545
00:44:40,160 --> 00:44:42,500
Say, what are you, a troublemaker

546
00:44:42,500 --> 00:44:44,950
You sure you ain't one of them labour fakes?

547
00:44:45,040 --> 00:44:47,030
l... l swear l ain't, mister.

548
00:44:47,130 --> 00:44:51,790
Don't you go around here trying to stir up any trouble.

549
00:44:51,880 --> 00:44:55,880
l tried to tell you folks what it took me a year to find out.

550
00:44:55,970 --> 00:45:00,090
Took two kids dead. Took my wife dead to show me.

551
00:45:00,090 --> 00:45:02,340
But nobody could tell me neither.

552
00:45:02,430 --> 00:45:06,050
l can't tell you about them little fellows laying in the tent

553
00:45:06,050 --> 00:45:07,640
with their bellies swelled out

554
00:45:07,730 --> 00:45:09,970
and just skin over their bones.

555
00:45:10,070 --> 00:45:13,020
Shivering and whining like pups.

556
00:45:13,110 --> 00:45:15,600
And me running around looking for work.

557
00:45:15,700 --> 00:45:18,740
Not for money. Not for wages.

558
00:45:18,830 --> 00:45:21,910
Just for a cup of flour and a spoon of lard.

559
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:24,120
Then the coroner come.

560
00:45:24,210 --> 00:45:26,650
''Them children died of heart failure,''

561
00:45:26,650 --> 00:45:29,370
he said. He put it down in his paper.

562
00:45:29,470 --> 00:45:31,540
Heart failure?

563
00:45:31,640 --> 00:45:36,300
And their little bellies stuck out like a pig bladder.

564
00:45:38,140 --> 00:45:41,560
Well, it's late. l got to get some sleep.

565
00:45:43,480 --> 00:45:45,020
Well...

566
00:45:45,110 --> 00:45:47,190
Good night, folks.

567
00:46:00,170 --> 00:46:04,380
Suppose he's telling the truth, that fella?

568
00:46:04,470 --> 00:46:07,000
He's telling the truth.

569
00:46:07,090 --> 00:46:09,500
The truth for him.

570
00:46:09,600 --> 00:46:12,090
He wasn't making it up.

571
00:46:12,180 --> 00:46:14,940
Was it the truth for us?

572
00:46:15,020 --> 00:46:17,100
l don't know.

573
00:47:07,460 --> 00:47:09,660
l got to get out, l tell you.

574
00:47:09,750 --> 00:47:11,830
l gotta get out now.

575
00:47:21,060 --> 00:47:25,140
-You folks aim to buy anything? -We want some gas, mister.

576
00:47:25,230 --> 00:47:28,760
-Got any money? -What do you think? We're begging?

577
00:47:28,860 --> 00:47:31,170
l just asked, that's all.

578
00:47:31,180 --> 00:47:33,730
Well, ask right. You ain't talking to bums.

579
00:47:33,820 --> 00:47:36,740
All in the world l done was ask.

580
00:47:37,530 --> 00:47:38,980
What kind of pie you got?

581
00:47:38,980 --> 00:47:41,580
Banana, pineapple, chocolate and apple.

582
00:47:41,660 --> 00:47:44,330
Cut me a hunk of that banana cream and a cup ofjava.

583
00:47:44,420 --> 00:47:46,910
-Make it two. -Two it is.

584
00:47:47,000 --> 00:47:49,710
Seen any good etchings lately, Bill?

585
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:51,460
Well, this one ain't bad.

586
00:47:51,460 --> 00:47:54,790
A little kid comes late for school and the teacher says...

587
00:47:54,890 --> 00:47:56,970
Cheese it.

588
00:47:57,810 --> 00:48:01,180
Could you see your way clear to sell us a loaf of bread, ma'am?

589
00:48:01,270 --> 00:48:04,560
This ain't a grocery store. We got bread to make sandwiches.

590
00:48:04,650 --> 00:48:08,860
l know, ma'am. Only, it's for an old lady.

591
00:48:08,950 --> 00:48:12,910
No teeth. Got to soften it with water so she can chew it,

592
00:48:12,910 --> 00:48:13,990
and she's hungry.

593
00:48:14,080 --> 00:48:16,620
Why don't you buy a sandwich? We got nice ones.

594
00:48:16,710 --> 00:48:19,450
Well, l sure would like to do that, ma'am.

595
00:48:19,450 --> 00:48:22,660
But the fact is, we ain't got but a dime for it.

596
00:48:22,750 --> 00:48:25,330
lt's all figured out -l mean for the trip.

597
00:48:25,420 --> 00:48:27,570
You can't buy no loaf of bread for a dime.

598
00:48:27,570 --> 00:48:29,720
We only got 15-cent loaves.

599
00:48:29,810 --> 00:48:33,170
-Give him the bread. -We'll run out before the bread truck comes!

600
00:48:33,270 --> 00:48:35,760
All right, then we run out!

601
00:49:00,380 --> 00:49:02,540
This here's a 15-cent loaf.

602
00:49:02,640 --> 00:49:04,230
Well, would you...

603
00:49:04,230 --> 00:49:07,100
could you see your way to cutting off ten cents worth?

604
00:49:07,180 --> 00:49:11,840
-Give him the loaf. -No, sir. We wanna buy ten cents worth.

605
00:49:11,940 --> 00:49:14,430
Go on, it's yesterday's bread.

606
00:49:14,520 --> 00:49:17,980
Go ahead. Bert says to take it.

607
00:49:18,070 --> 00:49:20,820
Well, it may sound funny,

608
00:49:20,820 --> 00:49:23,580
being so tight, but we got 1,000 miles to go

609
00:49:23,660 --> 00:49:26,410
and we don't know if we'll make it.

610
00:49:33,050 --> 00:49:35,250
ls them penny candies, ma'am?

611
00:49:35,340 --> 00:49:38,130
-Which ones? -There, them stripy ones.

612
00:49:38,220 --> 00:49:40,130
Oh, them.

613
00:49:40,220 --> 00:49:43,010
Well... no.

614
00:49:43,100 --> 00:49:47,260
-Them's two for a penny. -Give us two then, ma'am.

615
00:49:51,400 --> 00:49:54,150
Go on, take 'em, take 'em.

616
00:49:54,240 --> 00:49:56,310
Thank you, ma'am.

617
00:50:03,920 --> 00:50:07,870
-Them ain't two-for-a-cent candy. -What's it to you?

618
00:50:07,960 --> 00:50:11,090
Them's a-nickel-apiece candy.

619
00:50:11,090 --> 00:50:14,220
We better get going. We're dropping time.

620
00:50:18,560 --> 00:50:21,180
-So long. -Wait a minute, you got change coming.

621
00:50:21,270 --> 00:50:23,350
What's it to you?

622
00:50:30,990 --> 00:50:32,860
Bert.

623
00:50:34,450 --> 00:50:36,330
Look.

624
00:50:37,460 --> 00:50:39,530
Truck drivers.

625
00:51:18,960 --> 00:51:21,170
-Where you going? -California.

626
00:51:21,260 --> 00:51:23,470
How long you plan to be in Arizona?

627
00:51:23,470 --> 00:51:24,670
No longer than to get across.

628
00:51:24,760 --> 00:51:27,680
-Got any plants? -No, no plants.

629
00:51:30,600 --> 00:51:32,240
OK, go ahead, but keep moving.

630
00:51:32,240 --> 00:51:34,020
We aim to.

631
00:52:55,660 --> 00:53:00,790
Well, there she is, folks. The land of milk and honey.

632
00:53:00,880 --> 00:53:02,950
California.

633
00:53:07,590 --> 00:53:10,170
Well, if that's what we came out here for...

634
00:53:10,260 --> 00:53:13,430
Connie, maybe it's nice on the other side

635
00:53:13,430 --> 00:53:16,100
Them picture postcards, they was real pretty.

636
00:53:16,180 --> 00:53:19,520
There, Grandma. There's California.

637
00:53:26,070 --> 00:53:29,690
Let's get going. She don't look so tough to me, eh, John?

638
00:53:29,790 --> 00:53:31,860
Well, l don't know.

639
00:53:44,890 --> 00:53:46,960
Hold on!

640
00:54:36,150 --> 00:54:40,820
-Ain't too cold, is she, Tom? -No, it's fine when you get in, Pa.

641
00:54:40,910 --> 00:54:44,110
Come on, John. Let's give her a whirl.

642
00:54:54,680 --> 00:54:57,000
This is supposed to be good for you, John.

643
00:54:57,100 --> 00:55:01,340
-Come on, Pa, before she floats away. -Here we come.

644
00:55:06,230 --> 00:55:09,070
-You people got a lot of nerve. -What do you mean?

645
00:55:09,150 --> 00:55:12,690
-Crossing the desert in ajalopy like this. -You been across?

646
00:55:12,780 --> 00:55:16,730
Sure, plenty. But never in no wreck like that.

647
00:55:16,830 --> 00:55:19,370
lf we break down, maybe somebody'd give us a hand.

648
00:55:19,460 --> 00:55:20,310
Well, maybe,

649
00:55:20,310 --> 00:55:23,290
but l'd hate to be doing it. Takes more nerve than l got.

650
00:55:23,380 --> 00:55:25,930
Don't take no nerve to do something,

651
00:55:25,930 --> 00:55:29,630
ain't nothing else you can do. Hope she holds.

652
00:55:42,030 --> 00:55:43,900
Grandpa.

653
00:55:49,660 --> 00:55:52,360
l want Grandpa.

654
00:55:54,830 --> 00:55:58,040
l want... Grandpa.

655
00:55:59,050 --> 00:56:01,750
Don't you fret now.

656
00:56:05,180 --> 00:56:08,680
Don't you fret now, Grandma.

657
00:56:09,140 --> 00:56:11,350
-Everybody set back there? -Yeah.

658
00:56:11,430 --> 00:56:13,560
Here we go.

659
00:56:13,650 --> 00:56:15,930
Thank you very much.

660
00:56:23,950 --> 00:56:26,810
Holy Moses, what a hard-looking outfit.

661
00:56:26,810 --> 00:56:28,580
All them Okies is hard-looking.

662
00:56:28,660 --> 00:56:32,250
Boy, but l'd hate to hit that desert in ajalopy like that.

663
00:56:32,330 --> 00:56:37,540
You and me got sense. Them Okies got no sense and no feeling.

664
00:56:37,630 --> 00:56:41,840
They ain't human. No human being would live the way they do.

665
00:56:41,930 --> 00:56:45,710
A human being couldn't stand to be so miserable.

666
00:56:45,810 --> 00:56:48,930
Just don't know any better, l guess.

667
00:57:09,540 --> 00:57:14,010
What a place. How would you like to walk across it?

668
00:57:14,090 --> 00:57:17,630
People done it. lf they could, we could.

669
00:57:17,720 --> 00:57:20,210
Lots must have died, too.

670
00:57:21,180 --> 00:57:23,470
Well, we ain't out of it yet.

671
00:57:27,650 --> 00:57:31,230
-This here's the desert. We're right in it. -l wish it was day.

672
00:57:31,320 --> 00:57:36,150
Tom said if it was day, it'd cut the gizzard right out of you.

673
00:57:36,240 --> 00:57:39,330
l seen a picture once and there was bones everywhere.

674
00:57:39,410 --> 00:57:44,040
-Man bones? -Some, l guess. But mostly cow bones.

675
00:57:44,130 --> 00:57:48,080
l sure would like to see some of them man bones.

676
00:57:52,850 --> 00:57:55,880
Grandpa. l want Grandpa.

677
00:57:55,980 --> 00:57:59,430
Yes. Now everything's going to be all right.

678
00:57:59,520 --> 00:58:05,440
We got to get across, Grandma. The family's got to get across.

679
00:58:14,830 --> 00:58:19,990
Seems like we wasn't never doing nothing but moving. l'm tired.

680
00:58:20,090 --> 00:58:22,160
Women's always tired.

681
00:58:23,970 --> 00:58:27,300
You ain't... you ain't sorry, are you, honey?

682
00:58:27,390 --> 00:58:29,340
No, but...

683
00:58:29,430 --> 00:58:32,420
But you seen that advertisement

684
00:58:32,420 --> 00:58:34,090
in the Spicy Western Story magazine.

685
00:58:34,190 --> 00:58:35,760
Don't pay nothing.

686
00:58:35,760 --> 00:58:39,810
Just send them the coupon and you're a radio expert.

687
00:58:39,900 --> 00:58:42,280
Nice clean work.

688
00:58:42,360 --> 00:58:44,520
But we can still do it, honey.

689
00:58:44,620 --> 00:58:49,200
l ought to done it then, not come on any trip like this.

690
00:59:12,270 --> 00:59:15,310
-What's this here? -Agricultural inspection.

691
00:59:15,400 --> 00:59:18,400
We gotta go over your stuff. Got any vegetables or seed?

692
00:59:18,490 --> 00:59:19,650
No.

693
00:59:19,740 --> 00:59:22,530
We gotta look over your stuff. You gotta unload.

694
00:59:22,620 --> 00:59:25,700
Unload? Holy Moses.

695
00:59:35,840 --> 00:59:38,840
You'll have to get out while we unload for inspection.

696
00:59:38,930 --> 00:59:42,070
We got a sick old lady.

697
00:59:42,070 --> 00:59:43,870
We got to get her to a doctor.

698
00:59:43,870 --> 00:59:44,770
We can't wait. You can't make us wait.

699
00:59:44,850 --> 00:59:46,930
Yeah? Well, we gotta look you over.

700
00:59:47,020 --> 00:59:49,900
Well, l swear we ain't got anything. l swear it.

701
00:59:49,990 --> 00:59:52,310
Grandma's awful sick.

702
00:59:52,410 --> 00:59:54,480
Look.

703
00:59:58,160 --> 01:00:00,240
You wasn't fooling.

704
01:00:01,790 --> 01:00:05,740
-You swear you got no fruit or vegetables? -No, l swear it.

705
01:00:05,840 --> 01:00:09,000
Go ahead. Get a doctor at Barstow. That's just eight miles.

706
01:00:09,090 --> 01:00:11,170
But don't stop or get off. Understand?

707
01:00:11,260 --> 01:00:13,880
-OK, Cap. Much obliged. -Thanks.

708
01:00:41,550 --> 01:00:43,960
Ma! Grandma! Look!

709
01:00:55,560 --> 01:00:56,940
There she is. There she is.

710
01:00:56,940 --> 01:01:01,110
l never knowed there was anything like her.

711
01:01:01,200 --> 01:01:05,030
-Will you look at her. -Look yonder, John.

712
01:01:07,910 --> 01:01:10,370
Wonder if them's orange trees, John?

713
01:01:10,370 --> 01:01:11,610
Look like orange trees to me.

714
01:01:11,710 --> 01:01:14,580
-They sure are pretty, whatever they are. -lndeed.

715
01:01:14,670 --> 01:01:17,330
Look at them haystacks.

716
01:01:17,330 --> 01:01:19,000
l bet we sure could have fun playing over there.

717
01:01:19,090 --> 01:01:22,760
Pretty, ain't it? Mighty pretty. Tom.

718
01:01:22,850 --> 01:01:26,380
Where's Ma? l want Ma to see this. Look, Ma. Come here, Ma.

719
01:01:26,480 --> 01:01:28,550
Come on. Come on.

720
01:01:30,900 --> 01:01:33,900
-You sick, Ma? -You say we got across?

721
01:01:33,980 --> 01:01:35,810
Look.

722
01:01:35,900 --> 01:01:38,360
Oh, thank God.

723
01:01:38,450 --> 01:01:41,730
And we're still together. Most of us.

724
01:01:43,160 --> 01:01:45,290
Didn't you sleep none?

725
01:01:45,370 --> 01:01:48,290
-Was Grandma bad? -Grandma's dead.

726
01:01:49,750 --> 01:01:51,500
When?

727
01:01:51,590 --> 01:01:54,400
Since before they stopped us last night.

728
01:01:54,400 --> 01:01:57,210
That's why you didn't want them to look?

729
01:01:57,300 --> 01:02:01,970
l was afraid they'd stop us and we wouldn't get across.

730
01:02:02,980 --> 01:02:05,520
l told Grandma.

731
01:02:05,600 --> 01:02:07,410
l told her when she was dying.

732
01:02:07,410 --> 01:02:10,600
l told her the family had to get across.

733
01:02:10,690 --> 01:02:15,360
l told her we couldn't take no chance on being stopped.

734
01:02:17,450 --> 01:02:19,530
So it's all right.

735
01:02:20,370 --> 01:02:24,200
She'll get buried where it's nice and green,

736
01:02:24,200 --> 01:02:26,250
trees and flowers all around.

737
01:02:29,010 --> 01:02:34,000
She got to lay her head down in California after all.

738
01:02:47,700 --> 01:02:48,890
Whoa.

739
01:02:53,960 --> 01:02:57,160
How far you figure you going to get that way, pushing?

740
01:02:57,250 --> 01:02:59,710
Right here. We run out of gas.

741
01:03:00,550 --> 01:03:03,070
Where's the best place to get work around here?

742
01:03:03,070 --> 01:03:04,960
Don't matter what kind either.

743
01:03:05,050 --> 01:03:09,260
lf l've seen one of them things, l've seen 10,000 of 'em.

744
01:03:09,350 --> 01:03:12,720
-Why? Ain't it no good? -Not here. Not now.

745
01:03:12,810 --> 01:03:15,730
There was some picking around here about a month ago,

746
01:03:15,730 --> 01:03:17,190
but it's all moved south.

747
01:03:17,270 --> 01:03:20,360
-What part of Oklahoma you from, anyhow? -Sallisaw.

748
01:03:20,440 --> 01:03:21,800
Sallisaw?

749
01:03:21,800 --> 01:03:24,820
l come out from Cherokee County myself about two years ago.

750
01:03:24,910 --> 01:03:27,450
-Cherokee County! Gee! -Oh, boy!

751
01:03:28,290 --> 01:03:32,830
-Connie's folks are from Cherokee County. -You don't say!

752
01:03:32,920 --> 01:03:36,580
All right, all right. Let's don't go into it.

753
01:03:36,670 --> 01:03:38,540
What l gotta tell you is this:

754
01:03:38,540 --> 01:03:42,300
don't try to park in town tonight. Just go on out to that camp.

755
01:03:42,390 --> 01:03:44,960
lf l catch you in town after dark l gotta lock you up.

756
01:03:45,060 --> 01:03:49,430
-But what are we gonna do? -Well, pop, thatjust ain't up to me.

757
01:03:49,520 --> 01:03:51,500
l don't mind telling you

758
01:03:51,500 --> 01:03:52,920
the guy they ought to lock up is

759
01:03:52,920 --> 01:03:55,480
the guy that sent them things out.

760
01:03:59,110 --> 01:04:01,320
How many, folks?

761
01:04:01,410 --> 01:04:03,280
One.

762
01:05:30,510 --> 01:05:34,130
Sure don't look none too prosperous.

763
01:05:34,230 --> 01:05:38,010
-Wanna go somewhere else? -On a gallon of gas?

764
01:05:38,110 --> 01:05:41,860
Let's set up the tent and maybe l can fix some stew.

765
01:06:06,520 --> 01:06:10,350
l could break up some brush if you want me, ma'am.

766
01:06:10,440 --> 01:06:13,940
-You wanna be asked to eat, don't you? -Yes, ma'am.

767
01:06:14,020 --> 01:06:15,870
Didn't you have no breakfast?

768
01:06:15,870 --> 01:06:18,150
No, ma'am. They ain't no work hereabouts.

769
01:06:18,240 --> 01:06:21,210
Pa's been trying to sell some stuff to get gas,

770
01:06:21,210 --> 01:06:22,570
so as we can get along.

771
01:06:22,660 --> 01:06:26,410
Didn't none of these have no breakfast?

772
01:06:26,500 --> 01:06:30,880
l did. Me and my brother did. We ate good.

773
01:06:30,960 --> 01:06:35,340
-Well, you ain't hungry then, are you? -We ate good.

774
01:06:35,430 --> 01:06:37,500
l'm glad some of you ain't hungry.

775
01:06:37,500 --> 01:06:39,380
There won't be enough to go all around.

776
01:06:39,470 --> 01:06:42,050
Aw, he was braggin'. Know what he done?

777
01:06:42,140 --> 01:06:45,010
Last night, come out and say they got chicken to eat.

778
01:06:45,100 --> 01:06:47,500
Well, l looked in whilst

779
01:06:47,500 --> 01:06:51,110
they was eating and it was fried dough, just like everybody else.

780
01:06:51,190 --> 01:06:53,350
Ma? How about it?

781
01:06:53,450 --> 01:06:57,110
Well, l don't know what to do. l've got to feed the family...

782
01:06:57,200 --> 01:07:00,540
What am l gonna do about all these here?

783
01:07:07,090 --> 01:07:09,580
Give this to Ruthie.

784
01:07:15,890 --> 01:07:18,380
There you are, John.

785
01:07:28,240 --> 01:07:31,160
Here, Tom. You take it. l ain't hungry.

786
01:07:31,240 --> 01:07:34,550
What do you mean? You ain't ate today.

787
01:07:34,550 --> 01:07:35,570
l know, but l got a stomachache.

788
01:07:35,660 --> 01:07:37,990
l ain't hungry.

789
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:40,940
Take that plate in the tent and you eat it.

790
01:07:40,950 --> 01:07:44,340
lt wouldn't be no use. l'd still see them.

791
01:07:46,180 --> 01:07:48,550
You get! Go on now. Get!

792
01:07:48,640 --> 01:07:54,010
You ain't doing no good. There ain't enough for you anyway.

793
01:07:54,100 --> 01:07:56,390
Go on... now.

794
01:08:08,790 --> 01:08:11,110
You can't send them away.

795
01:08:11,200 --> 01:08:14,620
Here. Take your plates and go inside.

796
01:08:15,460 --> 01:08:16,910
Now look, all you little fellas.

797
01:08:16,910 --> 01:08:20,120
You each go and get you a nice flat stick

798
01:08:20,220 --> 01:08:23,090
and l'll put what's left for you. Huh?

799
01:08:23,180 --> 01:08:25,250
Now get.

800
01:08:29,770 --> 01:08:33,520
l don't know whether l'm doing right or not.

801
01:08:34,520 --> 01:08:39,480
Get inside! Get inside, everybody, and stay inside!

802
01:08:39,570 --> 01:08:43,410
Lady's gonna feed us. Get yourself a tin can.

803
01:08:56,930 --> 01:09:00,880
-Come on. Give me some. -You're taking too much.

804
01:09:23,250 --> 01:09:25,330
You men wanna work?

805
01:09:26,670 --> 01:09:28,910
Sure we wanna work. Where's it at?

806
01:09:29,010 --> 01:09:32,420
Tovaris County. Fruit's opening up. Need a lot of fruit pickers.

807
01:09:32,510 --> 01:09:36,590
-You doing the hiring? -Well, l'm contracting the land.

808
01:09:36,680 --> 01:09:38,610
What you paying?

809
01:09:38,610 --> 01:09:42,470
Well, can't tell exactly yet. 30 cents, l guess.

810
01:09:42,560 --> 01:09:45,770
Why can't you tell? You took the contract, didn't you?

811
01:09:45,860 --> 01:09:48,020
That's true, but it's keyed to the price.

812
01:09:48,110 --> 01:09:51,400
Might be a little more, might be a little less.

813
01:09:51,490 --> 01:09:54,110
All right, mister. l'll go.

814
01:09:54,200 --> 01:09:56,620
Just show us your licence to contract.

815
01:09:56,620 --> 01:09:57,740
Then you make out an order.

816
01:09:57,830 --> 01:09:59,530
Where, when and how much you're gonna pay.

817
01:09:59,530 --> 01:10:01,420
You sign it and we'll go.

818
01:10:01,500 --> 01:10:04,540
Now listen, smart guy. l'll run my business my own way.

819
01:10:04,630 --> 01:10:08,520
l got work. lf you want to take it, OK.

820
01:10:08,520 --> 01:10:10,340
lf not, just sit here, that's all.

821
01:10:11,050 --> 01:10:13,760
Twice now l've fell for that line.

822
01:10:13,850 --> 01:10:15,870
Maybe he needs 1,000 men.

823
01:10:15,870 --> 01:10:19,520
So he gets 5,000 there and he'll pay 15 cents an hour.

824
01:10:19,610 --> 01:10:22,560
You guys will have to take it because you'll be hungry.

825
01:10:22,650 --> 01:10:24,010
lf he wants to hire,

826
01:10:24,010 --> 01:10:26,190
let him write it out and say what he's gonna pay.

827
01:10:26,280 --> 01:10:27,820
Ask to see his licence.

828
01:10:27,820 --> 01:10:30,150
He ain't allowed to contract men without a licence.

829
01:10:30,250 --> 01:10:32,450
Hey, Joe.

830
01:10:32,540 --> 01:10:34,620
Agitator.

831
01:10:40,090 --> 01:10:44,220
-Ever see this guy before? -Seems like l have.

832
01:10:44,300 --> 01:10:47,110
Seems like l seen him hanging around a used-car lot

833
01:10:47,110 --> 01:10:48,880
that was busted into.

834
01:10:48,980 --> 01:10:52,430
Yep, that's the fella. Get in this car.

835
01:10:52,520 --> 01:10:56,140
-You got nothing on him. -Open your trap again and you'll go too.

836
01:10:56,480 --> 01:10:58,980
You don't wanna listen to troublemakers.

837
01:10:58,980 --> 01:11:01,690
You better all pack and come to Tovaris County.

838
01:11:01,780 --> 01:11:03,860
Come on, you.

839
01:11:35,860 --> 01:11:37,220
Give me that gun.

840
01:11:37,220 --> 01:11:39,730
Now get out of here. Go down to the willows and wait.

841
01:11:39,830 --> 01:11:43,330
-l ain't gonna run. -Why, the sheriff, he seen you, Tom.

842
01:11:43,420 --> 01:11:46,660
You wanna get fingerprinted?

843
01:11:46,660 --> 01:11:48,410
You wanna be sent back for breaking parole?

844
01:11:48,500 --> 01:11:51,000
l guess you're right.

845
01:11:51,930 --> 01:11:54,110
Hide in the willows.

846
01:11:54,110 --> 01:11:55,990
lf it's all right for you to come back,

847
01:11:55,990 --> 01:11:58,180
l'll give you four high whistles.

848
01:12:24,510 --> 01:12:25,870
What's going on here?

849
01:12:25,870 --> 01:12:28,840
This man of yours, he got tough so l hit him.

850
01:12:28,930 --> 01:12:32,040
Then he started shooting. Hit that woman there,

851
01:12:32,040 --> 01:12:33,340
so l hit him again.

852
01:12:33,430 --> 01:12:35,640
What did you do in the first place?

853
01:12:35,730 --> 01:12:37,800
l talked back.

854
01:12:39,060 --> 01:12:41,470
ls this the fella that hit you?

855
01:12:42,320 --> 01:12:45,490
-Don't look like him. -Oh, it was me all right.

856
01:12:45,570 --> 01:12:48,060
You just got smart with the wrong fella.

857
01:12:48,160 --> 01:12:50,450
Get in that car.

858
01:13:11,480 --> 01:13:13,830
This lady's bleeding to death.

859
01:13:13,830 --> 01:13:16,900
Boy, what a mess them .45s make.

860
01:13:16,980 --> 01:13:19,690
Better get the doc.

861
01:13:57,200 --> 01:13:59,490
Al.

862
01:13:59,580 --> 01:14:02,200
-You can come in now. -We gotta get out of here.

863
01:14:02,290 --> 01:14:03,910
Guy in the willows was telling me

864
01:14:03,910 --> 01:14:06,920
them pool room fellas figure on burning the camp out.

865
01:14:07,000 --> 01:14:10,290
We gotta get the truck loaded. Ma.

866
01:14:10,380 --> 01:14:12,260
Pa.

867
01:14:15,850 --> 01:14:19,590
What are you doing with the jack handle?

868
01:14:19,590 --> 01:14:21,300
Oh, she just got sassy, that's all.

869
01:14:21,390 --> 01:14:24,180
Fight it out later. We got to hustle. Where's Connie?

870
01:14:24,270 --> 01:14:27,940
Well, Tom, he's gone.

871
01:14:27,940 --> 01:14:29,270
He lit out this evening.

872
01:14:29,270 --> 01:14:30,280
Said he didn't know it was gonna be like this.

873
01:14:30,360 --> 01:14:33,610
-Glad to get shot of him. Never was no good. -Pa! Shh!

874
01:14:33,700 --> 01:14:36,190
How come l got to shh? Run out on us, didn't he?

875
01:14:36,290 --> 01:14:38,610
Cut it out, Pa. Help Al with the truck.

876
01:14:38,710 --> 01:14:44,210
Some of the fellas in town, they gonna burn out the camp tonight.

877
01:14:53,350 --> 01:14:57,100
Aw, don't fret, honey. You'll be all right.

878
01:15:00,440 --> 01:15:05,070
Tom, l just don't feel like nothing at all.

879
01:15:06,240 --> 01:15:09,440
Without him, l just don't want to live.

880
01:15:12,410 --> 01:15:16,460
He'll be back. We'll leave word for him.

881
01:15:16,540 --> 01:15:18,620
Just don't you worry.

882
01:15:31,600 --> 01:15:34,940
Get on. Ma, you and Rosasharn climb up.

883
01:16:12,150 --> 01:16:13,980
Ma.

884
01:16:14,070 --> 01:16:18,570
Maybe... maybe Connie gone to get some books to study up with.

885
01:16:18,660 --> 01:16:21,700
He going to be a radio expert, you know.

886
01:16:21,790 --> 01:16:24,110
Maybe he figured to surprise us.

887
01:16:24,210 --> 01:16:26,910
Maybe that's just what he done.

888
01:16:28,590 --> 01:16:30,910
Ma, there comes a time when a man gets mad.

889
01:16:31,010 --> 01:16:33,710
-You promised me... -l know, Ma. l'm trying to.

890
01:16:33,800 --> 01:16:37,140
lf there was a law, maybe we could take it. But it ain't the law.

891
01:16:37,220 --> 01:16:40,040
They're working on our spirits,

892
01:16:40,050 --> 01:16:41,850
trying to make us cringe and crawl, working on our decency.

893
01:16:41,940 --> 01:16:43,360
You promised, Tom.

894
01:16:43,360 --> 01:16:45,310
l know. l'm trying to, Ma. Honest l am.

895
01:16:45,400 --> 01:16:48,090
You gotta keep clear. The family's breaking up.

896
01:16:48,090 --> 01:16:49,100
You gotta keep clear.

897
01:16:49,190 --> 01:16:51,320
What's that? A detour?

898
01:17:01,080 --> 01:17:03,790
-Tom! Tom! Please! -Tom!

899
01:17:07,170 --> 01:17:10,880
Just where do you think you're going?

900
01:17:12,890 --> 01:17:16,010
We're strangers here, mister.

901
01:17:16,010 --> 01:17:18,350
We heard there was work in a place called Tovaris.

902
01:17:18,440 --> 01:17:20,990
Yeah? You're heading the wrong way.

903
01:17:20,990 --> 01:17:24,390
What's more, we don't want more Okies in this town.

904
01:17:24,480 --> 01:17:29,450
There ain't enough work here for them that's already here.

905
01:17:29,530 --> 01:17:33,030
-Which way's it at? -You turn right around and head north.

906
01:17:33,120 --> 01:17:38,660
And don't you come back until the cotton's ready. You understand?

907
01:18:01,200 --> 01:18:03,950
Pa. Let's try that other tyre.

908
01:18:04,580 --> 01:18:09,150
-You got another flat tyre, Tom? -What? Another one?

909
01:18:11,210 --> 01:18:14,130
Pa, get that spare back there?

910
01:18:31,730 --> 01:18:34,570
Ma, will you get the hell off there.

911
01:18:34,570 --> 01:18:37,070
This is gonna be heavy enough.

912
01:18:37,910 --> 01:18:40,440
Tell you, something's got to happen soon.

913
01:18:40,530 --> 01:18:43,730
We're down to our last day of grease

914
01:18:43,730 --> 01:18:46,410
and two days of flour and ten potatoes.

915
01:18:46,500 --> 01:18:50,790
And Rosasharn -we gotta remember she's gonna be due soon.

916
01:18:53,220 --> 01:18:55,790
-Morning. -Morning.

917
01:18:55,890 --> 01:18:57,830
You folks looking for work?

918
01:18:57,830 --> 01:19:00,510
We're looking even under boards for work.

919
01:19:00,600 --> 01:19:03,270
-Can you pick peaches? -We can pick anything.

920
01:19:03,350 --> 01:19:05,030
There's plenty of work about 40 miles up here,

921
01:19:05,030 --> 01:19:07,140
this side of Pixley.

922
01:19:07,230 --> 01:19:09,310
You turn east on 32,

923
01:19:09,310 --> 01:19:13,020
look for the Keene ranch. Tell them Spencer sent you.

924
01:19:13,110 --> 01:19:17,030
-Mister, we sure thank you. -Thank you.

925
01:19:17,120 --> 01:19:19,200
Come on, Ma.

926
01:19:41,150 --> 01:19:44,100
-What is it? A wreck? -Where do you think you're going?

927
01:19:44,190 --> 01:19:47,310
Fella named Spencer sent us. Said there was work.

928
01:19:47,400 --> 01:19:49,980
-Oh, you wanna work, huh? -Sure do.

929
01:19:50,070 --> 01:19:52,910
All right. Just pull up in line there.

930
01:19:53,000 --> 01:19:55,880
OK for this one. Take 'em through.

931
01:19:55,880 --> 01:19:56,990
What's the matter? What happened?

932
01:19:57,080 --> 01:20:02,590
Oh, a little trouble up there, but you'll get through all right.

933
01:20:05,930 --> 01:20:08,000
Go ahead.

934
01:20:17,650 --> 01:20:19,860
What do you think it is? A washout?

935
01:20:19,940 --> 01:20:22,950
l don't know what these cops have got to do with it,

936
01:20:22,950 --> 01:20:24,240
but l don't like it.

937
01:20:24,320 --> 01:20:28,990
These are our own people too. All of them. l don't like this.

938
01:20:29,080 --> 01:20:31,780
Get going. Stay in line.

939
01:20:33,000 --> 01:20:35,080
Go ahead.

940
01:20:38,670 --> 01:20:41,710
What are you gonna do, scab?

941
01:21:25,020 --> 01:21:27,100
Go on. Hurry up.

942
01:21:29,110 --> 01:21:31,810
Come on, come on, come on.

943
01:21:31,900 --> 01:21:34,030
Go on up there.

944
01:21:34,820 --> 01:21:36,900
Up the street there.

945
01:21:39,660 --> 01:21:42,150
Keep in line, up the street.

946
01:22:01,270 --> 01:22:03,350
Hold it, bud.

947
01:22:06,950 --> 01:22:09,620
-Wanna work? -Sure, but what is this?

948
01:22:09,700 --> 01:22:13,120
-None of your business. Name? -Joad.

949
01:22:13,200 --> 01:22:15,530
-How many men? -Four.

950
01:22:15,620 --> 01:22:18,620
-Women? -Two.

951
01:22:18,710 --> 01:22:20,670
-Kids? -Two.

952
01:22:20,750 --> 01:22:23,040
Can you all work?

953
01:22:23,130 --> 01:22:26,330
-Sure, l guess so. -OK. House 63.

954
01:22:26,430 --> 01:22:30,050
Wages, five cents a box. No bruised fruit.

955
01:22:30,140 --> 01:22:33,340
Move along. You go to work right away.

956
01:22:59,170 --> 01:23:01,250
Come on, honey.

957
01:23:03,970 --> 01:23:06,640
-Name? -Joad.

958
01:23:06,730 --> 01:23:08,970
Say, what is all this here?

959
01:23:09,060 --> 01:23:11,900
-Joad. Not here. -Licence?

960
01:23:11,980 --> 01:23:14,820
Oklahoma EL204.

961
01:23:14,900 --> 01:23:16,640
Don't check.

962
01:23:16,740 --> 01:23:19,900
Now, you look here. We don't want no trouble with you.

963
01:23:19,990 --> 01:23:23,110
Just do your own work

964
01:23:23,110 --> 01:23:26,240
and mind your business and you'll be all right.

965
01:23:26,330 --> 01:23:30,580
Sure do wanna make you feel at home here all right.

966
01:23:37,090 --> 01:23:39,380
-We going to live here, Ma? -Why, sure.

967
01:23:39,470 --> 01:23:43,140
This won't be so bad once we get her washed out.

968
01:23:43,230 --> 01:23:45,550
l liked the tent better.

969
01:23:45,650 --> 01:23:48,220
This has got a floor.

970
01:23:48,320 --> 01:23:51,230
Won't leak when it rains.

971
01:23:52,490 --> 01:23:55,410
Here. This might come in handy.

972
01:24:00,080 --> 01:24:02,320
-Name? -Still Joad.

973
01:24:02,410 --> 01:24:04,290
-How many? -Six. You all go on.

974
01:24:04,380 --> 01:24:07,790
Rosasharn and me will unload the truck.

975
01:24:55,690 --> 01:24:58,850
-Any more of them hamburgers, Ma? -No, there ain't.

976
01:24:58,940 --> 01:25:01,480
You made a dollar. That's a dollar's worth.

977
01:25:01,480 --> 01:25:02,940
Dollar's worth? That?

978
01:25:03,030 --> 01:25:05,360
They charge extra at that company store

979
01:25:05,360 --> 01:25:06,730
and there ain't no other place.

980
01:25:06,830 --> 01:25:09,030
Well, l ain't full.

981
01:25:09,120 --> 01:25:12,160
Tomorrow you'll get in a full day's work

982
01:25:12,160 --> 01:25:16,120
and a full day's pay and then we'll all have enough.

983
01:25:16,210 --> 01:25:18,900
You wouldn't thinkjust reaching up

984
01:25:18,900 --> 01:25:21,590
and picking would get you in the back.

985
01:25:21,680 --> 01:25:23,610
Think l'll walk out and find out

986
01:25:23,610 --> 01:25:25,540
what all that fuss outside the gate was.

987
01:25:25,640 --> 01:25:27,720
Anybody come with me?

988
01:25:27,720 --> 01:25:29,800
No, l think l'll set awhile, then go to bed.

989
01:25:29,890 --> 01:25:33,390
Think l'll look around and see if l can't meet me a girl.

990
01:25:33,480 --> 01:25:35,810
-Say, when l was your age, l... -Pa!

991
01:25:35,900 --> 01:25:37,610
Working on me what all that yelling was about.

992
01:25:37,610 --> 01:25:40,310
Got me all curious.

993
01:25:40,410 --> 01:25:42,980
l'll be back in a little while.

994
01:25:44,910 --> 01:25:47,910
Tom. Now, you be careful.

995
01:25:47,910 --> 01:25:49,410
Don't you go sticking your nose in anything.

996
01:25:49,500 --> 01:25:51,790
OK, Ma. Don't worry.

997
01:26:02,930 --> 01:26:05,550
Where do you think you're going?

998
01:26:05,640 --> 01:26:07,850
Thought l'd take a walk.

999
01:26:07,940 --> 01:26:10,670
ls there any law against that?

1000
01:26:10,670 --> 01:26:12,270
You can just turn around and walk back.

1001
01:26:12,360 --> 01:26:15,940
-You mean l can't even get out of here? -Not tonight you can't.

1002
01:26:16,030 --> 01:26:18,990
Do you want to walk back?

1003
01:26:18,990 --> 01:26:22,290
Or shall l whistle up some help and have you taken back?

1004
01:26:24,670 --> 01:26:27,370
l'll walk back.

1005
01:27:13,310 --> 01:27:15,380
-Evening. -Who are you?

1006
01:27:15,480 --> 01:27:18,150
-Just going past. -Know anybody around here?

1007
01:27:18,230 --> 01:27:20,940
No. Just going past, l tell you.

1008
01:27:24,200 --> 01:27:27,810
-Casy! -Well, if it ain't Tom Joad. Hiya, boy.

1009
01:27:28,700 --> 01:27:32,620
-l thought you was in jail. -No. They just run me out of town.

1010
01:27:32,710 --> 01:27:35,620
Come on in. Tom Joad.

1011
01:27:37,420 --> 01:27:39,520
This the fella you been talking about?

1012
01:27:39,520 --> 01:27:41,170
That's him. What are you doing?

1013
01:27:41,260 --> 01:27:43,500
Working, picking peaches.

1014
01:27:43,590 --> 01:27:45,980
l heard fellas shouting when we come in.

1015
01:27:45,980 --> 01:27:47,460
l came to find out what's going on.

1016
01:27:47,560 --> 01:27:51,140
-What's it about? -This here's a strike.

1017
01:27:51,230 --> 01:27:53,770
Five cents a box ain't much, but a fella can eat.

1018
01:27:53,860 --> 01:27:55,830
Five cents? They paying you five cents?

1019
01:27:55,830 --> 01:27:58,520
Sure. We made a buck since midday.

1020
01:27:58,610 --> 01:28:01,900
Looky, Tom. We come here to work.

1021
01:28:01,990 --> 01:28:04,140
They tell us it's gonna be five cents,

1022
01:28:04,140 --> 01:28:05,820
but there's a whole lot of us.

1023
01:28:05,910 --> 01:28:08,700
So the man says two and a half cents.

1024
01:28:09,540 --> 01:28:13,590
A fella can't even eat on that and if he's got kids...

1025
01:28:13,670 --> 01:28:15,750
So we says we won't take it.

1026
01:28:15,840 --> 01:28:19,130
So they drive us off. Now they're paying you five cents.

1027
01:28:22,260 --> 01:28:24,340
Dunno. Paying five now.

1028
01:28:24,430 --> 01:28:27,720
They'll get two and a half cents just the minute we're gone.

1029
01:28:27,810 --> 01:28:29,940
You know what that is.

1030
01:28:30,020 --> 01:28:34,150
One ton of peaches, picked and carried for a dollar.

1031
01:28:34,240 --> 01:28:38,020
That way you can't even buy enough food to keep you alive.

1032
01:28:38,120 --> 01:28:42,160
Tell 'em to come out with us, Tom. Them peaches is ripe.

1033
01:28:42,250 --> 01:28:46,990
Two days out and they'll pay us all five. Maybe seven.

1034
01:28:48,170 --> 01:28:50,480
They won't. They're getting five now.

1035
01:28:50,480 --> 01:28:51,710
That's all they care about.

1036
01:28:51,800 --> 01:28:53,220
But the moment they ain't strike-breaking,

1037
01:28:53,220 --> 01:28:55,220
they won't get no five.

1038
01:28:55,300 --> 01:28:57,590
Next thing you know you'll be out.

1039
01:28:57,590 --> 01:28:59,430
They got it all fixed down to a T.

1040
01:28:59,520 --> 01:29:02,050
Soon as the harvest is in you're a migrant worker.

1041
01:29:02,050 --> 01:29:03,390
Afterwards, just a bum.

1042
01:29:03,480 --> 01:29:07,150
Five they're getting now. That's all they're interested in.

1043
01:29:07,230 --> 01:29:10,440
l know what Pa would say. He'd say it's none of his business.

1044
01:29:10,530 --> 01:29:13,730
That's right. He'll have to take a beating before he'll know.

1045
01:29:13,830 --> 01:29:16,780
Take a beating? We was out of food.

1046
01:29:16,870 --> 01:29:20,210
Tonight we had meat -not much, but we had it.

1047
01:29:20,290 --> 01:29:23,180
You think Pa's gonna give up

1048
01:29:23,180 --> 01:29:24,790
his meat on account of some other fellas?

1049
01:29:24,880 --> 01:29:27,210
Rosasharn needs milk.

1050
01:29:27,300 --> 01:29:30,820
You think Ma's gonna starve

1051
01:29:30,820 --> 01:29:32,180
that baby just on account of fellas yelling outside a gate?

1052
01:29:32,260 --> 01:29:35,270
Tom, you gotta learn like l'm learning.

1053
01:29:35,350 --> 01:29:39,430
l don't know what's right yet myself, but l'm trying to find out.

1054
01:29:39,520 --> 01:29:42,890
That's why l can't ever be a preacher again.

1055
01:29:42,990 --> 01:29:45,190
Preacher's got to know.

1056
01:29:45,280 --> 01:29:47,770
l don't know.

1057
01:29:47,870 --> 01:29:49,690
l got to ask.

1058
01:29:49,790 --> 01:29:51,940
-l don't like it. -What's the matter?

1059
01:29:52,040 --> 01:29:55,420
l can't tell. Seems as though l hear something

1060
01:29:55,420 --> 01:29:57,540
and when l listen there ain't nothing to hear.

1061
01:29:57,630 --> 01:30:01,130
lt ain't out of the Question, you know.

1062
01:30:01,220 --> 01:30:04,580
Cops been telling us how they gonna beat us up and run us out.

1063
01:30:04,680 --> 01:30:07,900
Not them regular deputies, but them tin-seal men.

1064
01:30:07,900 --> 01:30:09,840
The ones they got for guards.

1065
01:30:09,930 --> 01:30:12,810
They figure l'm the leader cos l talk so much.

1066
01:30:12,900 --> 01:30:17,970
Turn out the light. Come outside. There's something here.

1067
01:30:22,240 --> 01:30:25,570
-What is it? -l don't know. Listen.

1068
01:30:25,660 --> 01:30:29,330
-Can't tell whether you hear it or not. -You hear it, Tom?

1069
01:30:29,420 --> 01:30:30,890
l hear it.

1070
01:30:30,890 --> 01:30:34,130
l think there's some guys coming this way. A lot of 'em.

1071
01:30:34,210 --> 01:30:39,720
-We gotta get outta here. -Down that way! Under the bridge span.

1072
01:30:59,450 --> 01:31:00,840
(man) There he is!

1073
01:31:00,840 --> 01:31:03,990
The one in the middle. The skinny one. Chuck! Alec! Get him!

1074
01:31:07,920 --> 01:31:11,000
You don't know what you're doing! You're helping starve kids.

1075
01:31:11,090 --> 01:31:13,580
-Aw, shut up, you dirty... -Casy!

1076
01:31:14,760 --> 01:31:17,970
-You've killed him! -Serves him right too.

1077
01:31:18,060 --> 01:31:20,130
Look out!

1078
01:31:28,740 --> 01:31:30,220
Boy, he's dead. He's good and dead.

1079
01:31:30,220 --> 01:31:32,600
Did you see the fella that done it?

1080
01:31:32,700 --> 01:31:35,980
l ain't sure, but l caught him across the face,

1081
01:31:35,980 --> 01:31:38,620
a trademark he won't get rid of in a hurry.

1082
01:31:51,890 --> 01:31:53,350
Ma?

1083
01:31:53,430 --> 01:31:56,850
Tom! Tom! Pa, wake up. Al, get the light.

1084
01:32:56,760 --> 01:32:59,250
-Anybody ask anything? -No, ma'am.

1085
01:32:59,340 --> 01:33:02,960
-Well, you stay by that door. -Yes, ma'am.

1086
01:33:04,770 --> 01:33:06,640
Tommy?

1087
01:33:07,810 --> 01:33:09,890
How's it feel?

1088
01:33:11,940 --> 01:33:15,060
Busted my cheek, but l can still see.

1089
01:33:17,360 --> 01:33:20,730
-What did you hear? -Looks like you've done it.

1090
01:33:22,540 --> 01:33:25,320
l thought so. Felt like it.

1091
01:33:27,210 --> 01:33:30,080
Folks ain't talking about much else.

1092
01:33:30,170 --> 01:33:32,840
They say they got posses out.

1093
01:33:32,920 --> 01:33:36,090
Talking about a lynching when they catch the fella.

1094
01:33:36,180 --> 01:33:38,470
They killed Casy first.

1095
01:33:38,560 --> 01:33:41,140
That ain't the way they're telling it.

1096
01:33:41,140 --> 01:33:43,020
They're saying you done it first.

1097
01:33:43,100 --> 01:33:45,430
Do they know...

1098
01:33:45,520 --> 01:33:48,060
what the fellow looks like?

1099
01:33:48,150 --> 01:33:51,070
They know he got hit in the face.

1100
01:33:53,660 --> 01:33:55,950
l'm sorry, Ma.

1101
01:33:56,040 --> 01:33:57,530
l didn't know

1102
01:33:57,530 --> 01:33:59,780
what l was doing any more than when you take a breath.

1103
01:33:59,870 --> 01:34:03,160
l didn't even know l was going to do it.

1104
01:34:03,250 --> 01:34:05,960
Oh, it's all right, Tommy.

1105
01:34:08,010 --> 01:34:10,680
l wished you didn't do it.

1106
01:34:10,760 --> 01:34:13,470
But you done what you had to do.

1107
01:34:13,560 --> 01:34:16,310
And l can't read no fault in you.

1108
01:34:20,560 --> 01:34:23,350
l'm going away tonight. l can't go putting this on you.

1109
01:34:23,440 --> 01:34:25,150
Tom.

1110
01:34:25,240 --> 01:34:28,490
There's a whole lot l don't understand.

1111
01:34:28,570 --> 01:34:31,780
But going away ain't going to ease us.

1112
01:34:33,660 --> 01:34:36,750
There was a time we was on the land.

1113
01:34:36,830 --> 01:34:39,700
There was a boundary to us then.

1114
01:34:40,420 --> 01:34:44,000
Old folks died off and little fellas come...

1115
01:34:44,090 --> 01:34:47,130
We was always one thing.

1116
01:34:47,220 --> 01:34:49,380
We was the family.

1117
01:34:50,230 --> 01:34:52,850
Kind of whole and clear.

1118
01:34:53,690 --> 01:34:56,690
But now we ain't clear no more.

1119
01:34:56,770 --> 01:35:00,060
They ain't nothing that keeps us clear.

1120
01:35:00,150 --> 01:35:03,940
Al, he's hankering and gibbeting to be off on his own.

1121
01:35:04,030 --> 01:35:07,070
Uncle John's just dragging around.

1122
01:35:07,910 --> 01:35:12,290
Your pa's lost his place. He ain't the head no more.

1123
01:35:13,590 --> 01:35:16,080
We're cracking up, Tom.

1124
01:35:17,260 --> 01:35:19,750
There ain't no family now.

1125
01:35:23,310 --> 01:35:25,930
And Rosasharn, she's gonna have her baby,

1126
01:35:25,930 --> 01:35:29,140
but it won't have no family.

1127
01:35:29,230 --> 01:35:32,350
l been trying to keep her going, but...

1128
01:35:34,610 --> 01:35:36,850
And Winfield.

1129
01:35:36,950 --> 01:35:39,620
What's he gonna be this way?

1130
01:35:39,700 --> 01:35:43,620
Growing up wild, and Ruthie too.

1131
01:35:44,500 --> 01:35:46,570
Just like animals.

1132
01:35:49,000 --> 01:35:51,710
Got nothing to trust.

1133
01:35:54,930 --> 01:35:57,000
Don't go, Tom.

1134
01:35:58,180 --> 01:36:00,260
Stay and help.

1135
01:36:01,310 --> 01:36:03,770
Help me.

1136
01:36:08,650 --> 01:36:13,360
OK, Ma. l shouldn't, l know l shouldn't, but OK.

1137
01:36:16,700 --> 01:36:19,740
Ma, here come a lot of people.

1138
01:36:19,830 --> 01:36:22,160
-(man) How many? -Ten of us.

1139
01:36:22,250 --> 01:36:24,490
House 25. The number's on the door.

1140
01:36:24,590 --> 01:36:28,670
-OK, mister. What you paying? -Two and a half cents.

1141
01:36:28,760 --> 01:36:32,840
Two and a half? Say, mister, a man can't make his dinner on that.

1142
01:36:32,930 --> 01:36:34,470
Take it or leave it.

1143
01:36:34,470 --> 01:36:36,210
There are 200 men coming in from the South

1144
01:36:36,210 --> 01:36:37,180
will be glad to get it.

1145
01:36:37,270 --> 01:36:40,060
-But how are we gonna eat? -Look...

1146
01:36:40,150 --> 01:36:42,720
l didn't set the price. lf you want it,

1147
01:36:42,730 --> 01:36:44,940
OK. lf you don't, turn around and beat it.

1148
01:36:45,030 --> 01:36:48,980
-Which way to house 25? -Straight up the street.

1149
01:36:57,670 --> 01:36:59,870
That Casy.

1150
01:36:59,960 --> 01:37:04,340
He might have been a preacher, but he seen things clear.

1151
01:37:04,420 --> 01:37:06,500
He was like a lantern.

1152
01:37:06,590 --> 01:37:09,090
He helped me to see things too.

1153
01:37:09,180 --> 01:37:12,300
Comes night, we'll get out of here.

1154
01:37:14,190 --> 01:37:16,260
Like a lantern.

1155
01:37:20,780 --> 01:37:23,270
-l'll start the car. -Yes.

1156
01:37:34,170 --> 01:37:36,250
All right, Tom.

1157
01:37:38,550 --> 01:37:40,630
Jump up. Jump up.

1158
01:37:44,350 --> 01:37:46,890
Just till we get distance. Then you can come out.

1159
01:37:46,980 --> 01:37:49,600
l'd hate to get trapped in here.

1160
01:37:49,690 --> 01:37:51,760
Get in, Ma.

1161
01:37:54,320 --> 01:37:57,400
-(man) Hey! Where you going? -We're going out.

1162
01:37:57,490 --> 01:38:00,570
-What for? -We got ajob offered. A good job.

1163
01:38:00,660 --> 01:38:01,900
Yeah?

1164
01:38:01,990 --> 01:38:04,570
Well, let's take a look at you.

1165
01:38:22,770 --> 01:38:26,220
-Wasn't there another fella with you? -You mean that hitchhiker?

1166
01:38:26,310 --> 01:38:29,090
Little short fella with a pale face?

1167
01:38:29,090 --> 01:38:30,260
l guess that's what he looked like.

1168
01:38:30,360 --> 01:38:33,110
We just picked him up on the way in.

1169
01:38:33,200 --> 01:38:35,800
He left when the rate dropped.

1170
01:38:35,800 --> 01:38:37,110
What did you say he looked like again?

1171
01:38:37,200 --> 01:38:39,530
Short fella. Pale face.

1172
01:38:39,620 --> 01:38:41,950
Was he bruised about the face this morning?

1173
01:38:42,040 --> 01:38:44,120
l didn't see nothing.

1174
01:38:44,210 --> 01:38:46,700
OK, go on.

1175
01:39:05,820 --> 01:39:10,610
-Going out for good? -Yeah. Going north. Got ajob.

1176
01:39:12,660 --> 01:39:14,530
OK.

1177
01:39:24,630 --> 01:39:28,550
-Done good, Al. You done real good. -Know where we're going?

1178
01:39:28,640 --> 01:39:32,910
lt don't matter. We got to go

1179
01:39:32,910 --> 01:39:34,890
and keep going till we get plenty of distance away from here.

1180
01:39:50,500 --> 01:39:53,120
Ouch! She's hotter than a heifer.

1181
01:39:53,210 --> 01:39:55,420
-What's up? -The fan belt's shot.

1182
01:39:55,500 --> 01:39:58,710
Sure picked a nice place for it too, didn't she?

1183
01:39:58,800 --> 01:40:02,170
-Got any gas? -About half a gallon.

1184
01:40:02,260 --> 01:40:04,930
Well, Ma, sure looks like we done her this time.

1185
01:40:05,010 --> 01:40:08,050
Lights up ahead. That might be a camp or something.

1186
01:40:08,140 --> 01:40:11,480
Looks like about a mile. Reckon she'll coast her, Al?

1187
01:40:11,560 --> 01:40:14,730
-Got to coast it. -Well, let's give her a whirl. Huh?

1188
01:40:14,820 --> 01:40:17,190
Come on, kids. Get in. John!

1189
01:40:54,450 --> 01:40:56,740
Did you hurt yourself, John?

1190
01:40:56,830 --> 01:40:58,700
No.

1191
01:41:00,160 --> 01:41:03,330
-You hit it too fast. -What's the idea of that?

1192
01:41:03,420 --> 01:41:06,250
Well, you see a lot of children play in here.

1193
01:41:06,340 --> 01:41:09,710
You can tell people to drive slow and they're liable to forget,

1194
01:41:09,800 --> 01:41:12,290
but once they hit that hump they don't forget.

1195
01:41:12,390 --> 01:41:14,880
-Got any room here for us? -You're lucky.

1196
01:41:14,970 --> 01:41:17,590
How do you do, ma'am? How are you?

1197
01:41:17,680 --> 01:41:19,840
How are you?

1198
01:41:19,940 --> 01:41:23,240
Down that line, turn to the left. You'll see it.

1199
01:41:23,240 --> 01:41:25,230
You'll be in number four sanitary unit.

1200
01:41:25,320 --> 01:41:29,150
-What's that? -Toilet, showers, washtubs.

1201
01:41:30,200 --> 01:41:32,570
We'll have washtubs with running water?

1202
01:41:32,660 --> 01:41:34,400
Yes, ma'am.

1203
01:41:34,490 --> 01:41:37,910
Camp committee will call on you in the morning, get you fixed.

1204
01:41:38,000 --> 01:41:40,950
-Cops? -No. No cops.

1205
01:41:41,040 --> 01:41:43,270
No, people here elect their own cops.

1206
01:41:43,270 --> 01:41:45,510
The ladies' committee'll call on you, ma'am.

1207
01:41:45,590 --> 01:41:47,750
Tell you about the children,

1208
01:41:47,750 --> 01:41:51,260
the schools and sanitary unit and who takes care of 'em.

1209
01:41:51,350 --> 01:41:54,270
Will you come inside and sign up?

1210
01:42:05,200 --> 01:42:08,150
Drive her on down, Al. l'll sign up.

1211
01:42:08,240 --> 01:42:11,160
Right this way. ln here.

1212
01:42:19,590 --> 01:42:22,710
Now, l don't want to seem inQuisitive, you understand,

1213
01:42:22,800 --> 01:42:28,260
but there's certain information l have to have. What's your name?

1214
01:42:28,350 --> 01:42:30,840
Joad. Tom Joad.

1215
01:42:30,940 --> 01:42:33,640
-J-O... -A-D.

1216
01:42:35,280 --> 01:42:37,650
And how many of you?

1217
01:42:38,490 --> 01:42:40,560
Eight. Now.

1218
01:42:43,080 --> 01:42:45,550
Uncle John, you don't look so good.

1219
01:42:45,550 --> 01:42:47,570
l ain't so good, but l'm coming.

1220
01:42:50,630 --> 01:42:51,910
Shove.

1221
01:42:52,000 --> 01:42:54,630
Camp site costs a dollar a week. You can work that out

1222
01:42:54,720 --> 01:42:58,760
carrying garbage, keeping the camp clean, things like that.

1223
01:42:58,850 --> 01:43:00,920
We'll work it out.

1224
01:43:01,010 --> 01:43:03,930
What's the committee you're talking about?

1225
01:43:04,020 --> 01:43:07,040
We have five sanitary units.

1226
01:43:07,040 --> 01:43:09,060
Each one elects a central committee man.

1227
01:43:09,150 --> 01:43:11,640
They make the laws and what they say goes.

1228
01:43:11,740 --> 01:43:15,220
You aiming to tell me

1229
01:43:15,220 --> 01:43:17,440
the fellas running the camp are just fellas camping here?

1230
01:43:17,530 --> 01:43:21,530
-That's the way it is. -And you say no cops?

1231
01:43:21,620 --> 01:43:24,710
No cop can come in here without a warrant.

1232
01:43:24,790 --> 01:43:27,370
l can't hardly believe it.

1233
01:43:27,460 --> 01:43:30,230
Camp l was in before, they burnt it out.

1234
01:43:30,230 --> 01:43:32,420
Deputies and some of them pool-room fellas.

1235
01:43:32,510 --> 01:43:36,420
They don't get in here. Sometimes the boys patrol the fences.

1236
01:43:36,510 --> 01:43:40,220
-Especially on dance nights. -You got dances too?

1237
01:43:40,310 --> 01:43:43,680
We have the best dances in the county, every Saturday night.

1238
01:43:43,770 --> 01:43:46,560
-Who runs this place? -The government.

1239
01:43:46,650 --> 01:43:51,110
-Why ain't there more like it? -You find out. l can't.

1240
01:43:52,780 --> 01:43:55,280
ls there anything like work around here?

1241
01:43:55,370 --> 01:43:57,030
l can't promise you that,

1242
01:43:57,030 --> 01:44:00,790
but there'll be a licensed agent here later if you want to talk.

1243
01:44:00,880 --> 01:44:03,830
That cut you have?

1244
01:44:03,920 --> 01:44:06,630
-Crate fell on me. -You'd better take care of it.

1245
01:44:06,720 --> 01:44:11,680
Store manager will give you something for it. See you later.

1246
01:44:11,760 --> 01:44:14,140
Ma's sure going to like it here.

1247
01:44:14,230 --> 01:44:19,560
She ain't been treated decent for a long while.

1248
01:44:21,110 --> 01:44:23,860
See you later.

1249
01:45:03,950 --> 01:45:08,080
-Winfield. l got something to show you. -What's the matter?

1250
01:45:08,160 --> 01:45:09,900
lt's some white things made out of dish stuff,

1251
01:45:09,900 --> 01:45:13,210
like in the catalogues. Come on, l'll show you.

1252
01:45:25,770 --> 01:45:29,600
Come on. Ain't nobody going to say anything.

1253
01:45:34,700 --> 01:45:38,360
There's where you wash your hands.

1254
01:45:38,450 --> 01:45:41,450
-What's these? -l reckon you stand in them little rooms.

1255
01:45:41,540 --> 01:45:44,180
And water comes down out of that little jigger up there.

1256
01:45:44,180 --> 01:45:45,200
You take a bath.

1257
01:45:45,290 --> 01:45:48,410
Oh, look. Just like in the catalogues.

1258
01:45:48,410 --> 01:45:49,460
Hey! Don't you go a-monkeyin'!

1259
01:45:51,340 --> 01:45:54,540
Now you done it! You busted it!

1260
01:45:54,640 --> 01:45:57,760
All l done was pull that string.

1261
01:46:16,200 --> 01:46:18,410
-Hiya, Mr Thomas. -Morning.

1262
01:46:18,500 --> 01:46:20,240
Morning.

1263
01:46:20,330 --> 01:46:22,330
Nice job.

1264
01:46:22,420 --> 01:46:26,100
Listen here. Maybe l'm going to talk myself out of my farm,

1265
01:46:26,100 --> 01:46:27,580
but l like you fellas.

1266
01:46:27,680 --> 01:46:30,210
You're good workers. So l'm going to tell you.

1267
01:46:30,300 --> 01:46:32,970
-You live over in the government camp? -Yes, sir.

1268
01:46:33,060 --> 01:46:36,470
-You have dances there every Saturday night. -We sure do.

1269
01:46:36,560 --> 01:46:40,260
-Well, look out next Saturday night. -What's the matter?

1270
01:46:40,360 --> 01:46:42,930
l'm head to the central committee. l got to know.

1271
01:46:43,030 --> 01:46:46,110
Well, don't tell l told you.

1272
01:46:46,200 --> 01:46:48,570
Listen.

1273
01:46:48,910 --> 01:46:54,200
"Citizens angered at red agitators burning other sQuatters' camps

1274
01:46:54,290 --> 01:46:56,830
and order agitators to leave the county."

1275
01:46:56,920 --> 01:46:59,960
Listen. What is these reds anyway?

1276
01:47:00,050 --> 01:47:01,560
Every time you turn around

1277
01:47:01,560 --> 01:47:03,830
somebody's calling somebody else a red.

1278
01:47:03,930 --> 01:47:06,600
What is these reds anyway?

1279
01:47:06,600 --> 01:47:08,300
l ain't talking about that, one way or another.

1280
01:47:08,390 --> 01:47:10,300
All l'm saying is there's going to be a fight

1281
01:47:10,300 --> 01:47:12,220
at the camp, Saturday night.

1282
01:47:12,310 --> 01:47:15,560
And there'll be deputies ready to go in.

1283
01:47:15,650 --> 01:47:18,110
Now go on with your work.

1284
01:47:18,190 --> 01:47:20,990
Maybe l've talked myself into trouble,

1285
01:47:20,990 --> 01:47:24,560
but you're folks like us and l like you.

1286
01:47:24,660 --> 01:47:26,950
We won't tell who told. Thank you.

1287
01:47:27,040 --> 01:47:31,200
-All right. -There ain't gonna be no fight either.

1288
01:47:36,880 --> 01:47:40,420
("She'll be Comin'Round the Mountain")

1289
01:47:45,690 --> 01:47:48,770
-Evening. Who did you say invited you? -Mr and Mrs Brown.

1290
01:47:48,860 --> 01:47:52,190
Oh, go right on in, folks. Go right on in. Hello, there.

1291
01:47:52,280 --> 01:47:55,030
Hi, Miz Jennings. How are you? Glad to see you.

1292
01:48:12,170 --> 01:48:14,080
-Hello. -Hello.

1293
01:48:14,180 --> 01:48:16,180
Going to the dance tonight? l can waltz.

1294
01:48:16,180 --> 01:48:18,920
Oh, that's nothing. Anybody can waltz.

1295
01:48:19,020 --> 01:48:21,890
-Not like me they can't. -You get going!

1296
01:48:21,980 --> 01:48:25,360
This girl's spoke for.

1297
01:48:25,360 --> 01:48:28,230
She's gonna be married and her man's coming for her. So get!

1298
01:48:34,120 --> 01:48:36,870
Hiya, Bill. Nice-looking girl you got there.

1299
01:48:36,950 --> 01:48:40,320
-Howdy, Mr Thomas, Mrs Thomas. -Watching out, ain't you?

1300
01:48:40,420 --> 01:48:43,670
Don't worry. There ain't gonna be no trouble.

1301
01:48:43,670 --> 01:48:45,760
l hope you know what you're talking about.

1302
01:48:45,840 --> 01:48:49,010
Evenin', boys. Who did you say invited you?

1303
01:48:49,090 --> 01:48:52,790
Fella named Jackson. Buck Jackson.

1304
01:48:52,890 --> 01:48:56,310
-OK. Have a good time. -Thanks.

1305
01:49:05,320 --> 01:49:08,490
-Them's our fellas. -How do you know?

1306
01:49:08,580 --> 01:49:10,240
Well...

1307
01:49:10,330 --> 01:49:12,740
Just got a feeling.

1308
01:49:12,830 --> 01:49:15,620
They're kind of scared too.

1309
01:49:15,710 --> 01:49:18,090
Follow 'em. Get hold of Jackson.

1310
01:49:18,090 --> 01:49:20,920
See if he knows them. l'll stay here.

1311
01:49:23,180 --> 01:49:26,010
-Hello. -Hello.

1312
01:49:26,100 --> 01:49:28,170
So long.

1313
01:49:32,350 --> 01:49:34,730
How do you do, Miz Joad?

1314
01:49:34,820 --> 01:49:37,310
My, you sure look pretty.

1315
01:49:46,490 --> 01:49:49,660
-Please to dance? -Thank you kindly, but she ain't well.

1316
01:49:49,750 --> 01:49:53,280
-Sort of poorly. -Thank you just the same.

1317
01:49:54,960 --> 01:49:57,040
Howdy do.

1318
01:50:04,390 --> 01:50:06,180
Hey, Jackson.

1319
01:50:06,270 --> 01:50:09,300
Look. Did you ever see them fellas before?

1320
01:50:14,610 --> 01:50:19,400
Sorry, neighbour, but we got to keep the camp clean.

1321
01:50:20,700 --> 01:50:23,280
l know one of 'em. Used to work with him.

1322
01:50:23,280 --> 01:50:25,000
l never asked him to the dance though.

1323
01:50:25,080 --> 01:50:26,660
All right.

1324
01:50:26,660 --> 01:50:29,830
Keep your eye on 'em. Just keep 'em in sight, that's all.

1325
01:50:37,260 --> 01:50:39,550
l seen them.

1326
01:50:39,550 --> 01:50:40,930
A car with five men parked down by the eucalyptus trees.

1327
01:50:41,020 --> 01:50:42,860
And another with four men on the main road,

1328
01:50:42,860 --> 01:50:44,890
and they got guns. l seen 'em.

1329
01:50:44,980 --> 01:50:49,210
Thank you, Willy. You done right good.

1330
01:50:49,210 --> 01:50:50,520
You can run along and dance now.

1331
01:50:50,610 --> 01:50:53,570
Well, sure looks like the fat's in the fire this time.

1332
01:50:53,660 --> 01:50:56,050
What them deputies wanna hurt the camp for?

1333
01:50:56,050 --> 01:50:57,530
How come they can't let us alone?

1334
01:50:57,620 --> 01:51:00,530
We ought to get some pickaxe handles...

1335
01:51:00,530 --> 01:51:03,000
No. That's just what they want.

1336
01:51:03,090 --> 01:51:05,290
No, sirree. lf they can get a fight going

1337
01:51:05,290 --> 01:51:07,750
they can call in the cops. Say we ain't orderly.

1338
01:51:07,840 --> 01:51:10,800
-They're here. We got 'em spotted. -Everything ready?

1339
01:51:10,890 --> 01:51:14,050
-There ain't gonna be trouble. -l don't want you hurting them.

1340
01:51:14,140 --> 01:51:17,640
Don't worry. Everything's arranged. Maybe nobody will see it.

1341
01:51:17,730 --> 01:51:22,720
Well, just don't use no sticks or no knives or no piece of iron.

1342
01:51:22,820 --> 01:51:26,400
lf you gotta sock 'em, sock 'em where they ain't going to bleed.

1343
01:51:40,670 --> 01:51:44,090
Gentlemen, hats off please. Thank you.

1344
01:52:01,110 --> 01:52:04,120
She's getting purdier every day, Ma.

1345
01:52:04,120 --> 01:52:07,370
Girl with a baby's always purdier.

1346
01:52:25,720 --> 01:52:29,340
-Come on, Ma, let's dance. -Oh, Tom. l can't.

1347
01:52:30,650 --> 01:52:32,720
Well, all right.

1348
01:52:36,950 --> 01:52:39,020
Tom, stop.

1349
01:52:46,420 --> 01:52:51,410
Come and sit by my side if you love me

1350
01:52:51,500 --> 01:52:56,470
Do not hasten to bid me adieu

1351
01:52:56,550 --> 01:53:01,470
But remember the Red River Valley

1352
01:53:01,560 --> 01:53:06,470
And the boy who had loved you so true

1353
01:53:18,120 --> 01:53:20,200
9.29. Let's go!

1354
01:53:26,210 --> 01:53:28,710
All right. 9.30. Here we go.

1355
01:53:33,810 --> 01:53:36,380
-All right. l'll dance with her. -You and who else?

1356
01:53:36,480 --> 01:53:38,720
Excuse me, Ma.

1357
01:53:46,860 --> 01:53:49,020
Open up! We're here about a riot!

1358
01:53:49,120 --> 01:53:53,410
-Riot? l don't see any riot. Who are you? -Deputy sheriffs.

1359
01:53:53,490 --> 01:53:56,830
-You got a warrant? -We don't need a warrant when there's a riot.

1360
01:53:56,920 --> 01:53:59,070
l don't know what you're going to do about it.

1361
01:53:59,070 --> 01:54:00,830
l don't hear or see any riot.

1362
01:54:00,920 --> 01:54:01,850
What's more,

1363
01:54:01,850 --> 01:54:05,830
l don't believe there is any riot. Look for yourself.

1364
01:54:17,360 --> 01:54:19,430
All right, let's go.

1365
01:55:12,510 --> 01:55:16,460
(man) Oklahoma... EL204.

1366
01:55:58,640 --> 01:56:01,220
You have no right to arrest without a warrant.

1367
01:56:01,310 --> 01:56:05,440
We'll have a warrant just as soon as we check with headQuarters.

1368
01:57:30,590 --> 01:57:32,830
Tommy.

1369
01:57:32,920 --> 01:57:35,840
Ain't you gonna tell me goodbye?

1370
01:57:42,930 --> 01:57:45,260
l didn't know, Ma.

1371
01:57:45,350 --> 01:57:47,230
l didn't know if l ought to.

1372
01:57:47,310 --> 01:57:50,560
-(Ruthie) Ma... -Hush, Ruthie.

1373
01:57:53,240 --> 01:57:55,320
Come outside.

1374
01:58:32,160 --> 01:58:34,590
There was some cops here tonight.

1375
01:58:34,590 --> 01:58:36,490
They was taking down licence numbers.

1376
01:58:36,580 --> 01:58:38,820
l guess somebody knows something.

1377
01:58:38,920 --> 01:58:42,670
l guess it had to come. Sooner or later.

1378
01:59:19,720 --> 01:59:22,210
Sit down for a minute.

1379
01:59:24,720 --> 01:59:27,340
l'd like to stay, Ma.

1380
01:59:27,430 --> 01:59:29,410
l'd like to be with you

1381
01:59:29,410 --> 01:59:33,390
and see your face when you and Pa get settled in some nice place.

1382
01:59:33,480 --> 01:59:36,190
l'd sure like to see you then.

1383
01:59:37,780 --> 01:59:41,940
But l won't never get that chance, l guess now.

1384
01:59:42,030 --> 01:59:43,990
l could hide you, Tommy.

1385
01:59:43,990 --> 01:59:46,280
l know you would, but l ain't gonna let you.

1386
01:59:46,370 --> 01:59:51,450
You hide somebody that's killed a guy and you're in trouble too.

1387
01:59:51,540 --> 01:59:54,210
All right, Tommy.

1388
01:59:54,300 --> 01:59:58,000
But what do you figure you're going to do?

1389
01:59:58,090 --> 02:00:01,130
You know what l've been thinking about?

1390
02:00:01,220 --> 02:00:03,550
About Casy.

1391
02:00:03,640 --> 02:00:06,100
About what he said.

1392
02:00:06,190 --> 02:00:08,430
What he done.

1393
02:00:08,520 --> 02:00:10,600
About how he died.

1394
02:00:11,780 --> 02:00:14,700
And l remember all of it.

1395
02:00:16,200 --> 02:00:18,770
He was a good man.

1396
02:00:18,870 --> 02:00:21,660
l've been thinking about us too.

1397
02:00:21,750 --> 02:00:25,760
About our people living like pigs and good,

1398
02:00:25,760 --> 02:00:27,920
rich land laying fallow.

1399
02:00:29,300 --> 02:00:32,280
Or maybe one guy with a million acres

1400
02:00:32,280 --> 02:00:35,260
and 100,000 farmers starving.

1401
02:00:35,350 --> 02:00:38,020
And l've been wondering if...

1402
02:00:38,100 --> 02:00:40,560
all our folks got together and yelled...

1403
02:00:40,650 --> 02:00:42,890
No. They'd drive you out and cut you down.

1404
02:00:42,980 --> 02:00:46,480
-Just like they done to Casy. -They're gonna drive me anyways.

1405
02:00:46,570 --> 02:00:50,980
Sooner or later they'd get me, for one thing if not for another.

1406
02:00:51,070 --> 02:00:53,230
Till then...

1407
02:00:53,330 --> 02:00:54,870
Tommy.

1408
02:00:54,950 --> 02:00:57,600
You're not aiming to kill nobody?

1409
02:00:57,600 --> 02:00:59,370
No, Ma. Not that. That ain't it.

1410
02:00:59,460 --> 02:01:01,580
lt's just...

1411
02:01:01,670 --> 02:01:05,920
Well, as long as l'm an outlaw anyways, maybe l can do something.

1412
02:01:06,010 --> 02:01:08,710
Maybe l can just find out something.

1413
02:01:08,800 --> 02:01:11,470
Just... scrounge around

1414
02:01:11,470 --> 02:01:15,220
and maybe find out what it is that's wrong.

1415
02:01:15,310 --> 02:01:20,770
Then see if they ain't something that can be done about it.

1416
02:01:20,860 --> 02:01:24,310
l ain't thought it all out clear, Ma. l...

1417
02:01:24,410 --> 02:01:26,900
l can't. l don't know enough.

1418
02:01:27,950 --> 02:01:30,740
How am l gonna know about you, Tommy?

1419
02:01:30,830 --> 02:01:34,000
Why, they could kill you and l'd never know.

1420
02:01:34,000 --> 02:01:36,500
They could hurt you. How am l gonna know?

1421
02:01:38,630 --> 02:01:42,580
Well, maybe it's like Casy says.

1422
02:01:43,640 --> 02:01:46,880
Fella ain't got a soul of his own,

1423
02:01:46,880 --> 02:01:49,430
just... a little piece of a big soul.

1424
02:01:49,520 --> 02:01:53,730
The one big soul that belongs to everybody.

1425
02:01:53,810 --> 02:01:55,770
Then...

1426
02:01:55,860 --> 02:01:59,030
-Then what, Tom? -Then it don't matter.

1427
02:01:59,860 --> 02:02:02,900
l'll be all around in the dark.

1428
02:02:02,990 --> 02:02:04,870
l'll be everywhere.

1429
02:02:04,950 --> 02:02:07,240
Wherever you can look.

1430
02:02:08,250 --> 02:02:13,870
Wherever there's a fight so hungry people can eat, l'll be there.

1431
02:02:14,510 --> 02:02:19,170
Wherever there's a cop beating up a guy, l'll be there.

1432
02:02:20,640 --> 02:02:24,470
l'll be in the way guys yell when they're mad.

1433
02:02:24,560 --> 02:02:28,550
l'll be in the way kids laugh

1434
02:02:28,550 --> 02:02:30,730
when they're hungry and they know supper's ready.

1435
02:02:31,490 --> 02:02:35,810
And when people are eating the stuff they raise...

1436
02:02:36,660 --> 02:02:40,030
living in the houses they build...

1437
02:02:40,120 --> 02:02:42,200
l'll be there too.

1438
02:02:46,130 --> 02:02:48,500
l don't understand it, Tom.

1439
02:02:49,750 --> 02:02:51,830
Me neither, Ma, but...

1440
02:02:53,050 --> 02:02:57,180
just something l've been thinking about.

1441
02:02:57,260 --> 02:03:00,100
Give me your hand, Ma.

1442
02:03:01,770 --> 02:03:03,400
Goodbye.

1443
02:03:03,480 --> 02:03:05,640
Goodbye, Tommy.

1444
02:03:05,730 --> 02:03:09,070
Later, when this is blowed over,

1445
02:03:09,150 --> 02:03:11,150
you'll come back.

1446
02:03:11,240 --> 02:03:13,320
Sure, Ma.

1447
02:03:14,490 --> 02:03:18,030
Tommy, we ain't the kissing kind, but...

1448
02:03:25,800 --> 02:03:28,260
Goodbye, Ma.

1449
02:03:28,340 --> 02:03:30,420
Goodbye, Tommy.

1450
02:03:38,730 --> 02:03:40,810
Tommy...

1451
02:04:19,360 --> 02:04:21,440
Goodbye.

1452
02:04:25,620 --> 02:04:28,030
Turn that up, Al, and get her rolled up.

1453
02:04:28,120 --> 02:04:30,160
-How you fixed, John? -Getting along.

1454
02:04:30,250 --> 02:04:32,330
Here.

1455
02:04:33,170 --> 02:04:35,160
Winfield, get on top, out of the way.

1456
02:04:35,260 --> 02:04:37,670
l don't see what you folks are hurrying so for.

1457
02:04:37,670 --> 02:04:40,080
They tell me there's 20 days' work up there.

1458
02:04:40,180 --> 02:04:42,720
Yes, sir, and we aim to get in all 20 of them.

1459
02:04:42,810 --> 02:04:46,010
-All ready, Ma? -Yes. How you feeling, Rosasharn?

1460
02:04:46,100 --> 02:04:49,850
All aboard, everybody. All aboard for Fresno.

1461
02:04:49,940 --> 02:04:52,510
Wait a minute. l'll give you a hand.

1462
02:04:52,610 --> 02:04:55,530
-Careful of her, now. -Easy, child.

1463
02:05:01,040 --> 02:05:03,110
She'll be all right.

1464
02:05:06,710 --> 02:05:10,040
-Watch her, John. -l'll take care of her.

1465
02:05:11,550 --> 02:05:14,670
-How are you fixed, Al? -All right, Pa.

1466
02:05:18,640 --> 02:05:20,720
Now, Ma.

1467
02:05:33,120 --> 02:05:35,190
-Goodbye. -Goodbye.

1468
02:05:37,000 --> 02:05:39,780
-Thanks a lot. Goodbye. -Goodbye.

1469
02:05:39,920 --> 02:05:42,040
Thanks, Mr Conway.

1470
02:05:44,880 --> 02:05:46,960
-Bye. -You be careful.

1471
02:06:06,660 --> 02:06:09,570
20 days work. Oh, boy!

1472
02:06:09,660 --> 02:06:12,270
l'll be glad to get my hands on some cotton.

1473
02:06:12,270 --> 02:06:14,570
That's the kind of picking l understand.

1474
02:06:14,670 --> 02:06:19,410
Maybe. Maybe 20 days' work and maybe no days' work.

1475
02:06:19,510 --> 02:06:21,510
We ain't got it till we get it.

1476
02:06:21,510 --> 02:06:23,370
What's the matter, Ma? Getting scared?

1477
02:06:23,470 --> 02:06:25,460
Scared. Huh!

1478
02:06:25,550 --> 02:06:28,340
l ain't never gonna be scared no more.

1479
02:06:28,430 --> 02:06:31,170
l was though. For a while it looked as though we was beat.

1480
02:06:31,170 --> 02:06:34,310
Good and beat.

1481
02:06:34,400 --> 02:06:37,730
Looked like we didn't have nobody in the world but enemies.

1482
02:06:37,820 --> 02:06:40,770
Like nobody was friendly no more.

1483
02:06:40,860 --> 02:06:44,400
Made me feel kind of bad and scared too.

1484
02:06:44,490 --> 02:06:47,410
Like we was lost and nobody cared.

1485
02:06:49,120 --> 02:06:52,210
You're the one that keeps us going, Ma.

1486
02:06:52,290 --> 02:06:55,300
l ain't no good no more and l know it.

1487
02:06:55,380 --> 02:06:58,320
Seems like l spend all my time these days thinking

1488
02:06:58,320 --> 02:07:00,290
how it used to be.

1489
02:07:00,390 --> 02:07:03,170
Thinking of home.

1490
02:07:03,260 --> 02:07:05,550
l ain't never gonna see it no more.

1491
02:07:05,640 --> 02:07:10,390
Well, Pa, a woman can change better than a man.

1492
02:07:10,480 --> 02:07:14,860
A man lives sort of, well, in jerks.

1493
02:07:14,940 --> 02:07:17,610
Baby's born or somebody dies, and that's ajerk.

1494
02:07:17,700 --> 02:07:21,610
He gets a farm or loses it, and that's ajerk.

1495
02:07:21,700 --> 02:07:25,320
With a woman it's all in one flow like a stream.

1496
02:07:25,420 --> 02:07:31,500
Little eddies and waterfalls, but the river, it goes right on.

1497
02:07:31,590 --> 02:07:34,380
A woman looks at it that way.

1498
02:07:36,600 --> 02:07:41,180
-Well, maybe, but we're sure taking a beating. -l know.

1499
02:07:42,060 --> 02:07:44,390
That's what makes us tough.

1500
02:07:44,480 --> 02:07:47,310
Rich fellas come up, and they die,

1501
02:07:47,310 --> 02:07:49,640
and their kids ain't no good and they die out.

1502
02:07:49,740 --> 02:07:53,730
But we keep coming. We're the people that live.

1503
02:07:53,820 --> 02:07:57,110
They can't wipe us out. They can't lick us.

1504
02:07:57,200 --> 02:08:01,580
We'll go on forever, Pa, cos we're the people.

